WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.386
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.930
Good afternoon.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:00.870
I'm Rafael Reif, MIT's
president number 17.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:06.340
To all of Paul's friends and
colleagues, to the Gray family

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.229
and to our beloved
Priscilla, it's

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:18.260
an honor to open the celebration
of Paul's remarkable life.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:22.430
All of us are gathered
here because of Paul.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.720
But in a larger sense,
I must point out,

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.760
that I'm here at
MIT because of him.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.010
That may be true for
many of you, too.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.830
For me, it's because
in the 1980s,

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.580
MIT was in a fierce
competition with

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.300
other top academic
institutions for leadership

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.600
in electrical engineering.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.040
As president, Paul
understood that MIT urgently

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.650
needed to create
what we ended up

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:03.010
calling MTL, the Microsystems
Technology Laboratories.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.140
Launching MTL had important
effect of keeping MIT

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.330
at the forefront of the field.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:15.410
And incidentally, it kept
me here on the faculty.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:19.180
So I'm a direct beneficiary
of Paul's clear vision,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.730
an exceptional ability
to get things done.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.320
As you can see from your
programs, our speakers today

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.800
and the program itself
explore many dimensions

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.650
of Paul's life story.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:39.490
So I will not attempt
to capture it myself.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:43.210
Instead, I will reflect
on a few lessons

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.375
he taught us all through the
eloquence of his example.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.860
We all know that Paul held
just about every job at MIT,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:00.370
including, finally, the
chairman of the corporation.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:03.030
After that, he went
back to the job

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.110
he loved the most, teaching.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.200
Naturally, he returned
to his home discipline,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.930
electrical engineering.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.209
At the time the
associate department head

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:21.320
for electrical engineering was
some microelectronics professor

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.090
from Venezuela
named Rafael Reif.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.400
Paul would always
come to my office

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.770
at the start of the semester,
this MIT legend, president

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.630
and then chairman.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:41.720
And he would ask me what
I wanted him to teach.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:47.200
I would always say, Paul,
what would you like to teach?

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.870
And he would always choose
6002, circuits and electronics.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.030
That's the first
academic subject

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:58.630
in electrical engineering.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.540
Paul used to say that it
was like learning to play

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.550
the scales for musicians.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:09.400
It's the foundation of
everything, he would say.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.330
But looking back now, I realize
what I should have said.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.960
I should have asked
Paul to teach me

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:24.090
how he managed to live his life
with such remarkable integrity.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:27.030
Of course, Paul was
profoundly honest.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:32.190
He lived by the highest
ethical and moral standards.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.850
But integrity, I mean,
went beyond that.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:42.180
Paul was, as a person,
integrated, unified,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.800
of a piece, like an
integrated circuit.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.700
There was a moral unity
to his whole life,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:54.120
a unity of purpose and values.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.620
He knew who he was
down to the core.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:04.210
That gave him deep personal
confidence in every situation.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:08.300
And he gave everyone
else perfect confidence

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.780
that he would always
do the right thing.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.380
And he always did.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.040
Over time, I had the
opportunity to ask

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.460
Paul for all kinds of advice.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.490
To be a new provost
or president here,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:30.410
facing your first big crisis,
and to have his counsel,

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.030
it was a gift.

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:35.630
All of us who came
after him in those jobs

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:40.410
felt incredibly
fortunate to know him.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:44.410
And there may never have been a
better manager of a university

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.280
than Paul Gray.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.930
His memory on every issue and
his knowledge of the budget

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:53.760
were humbling.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.780
I must tell you, though,
that the best advice he ever

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:00.140
gave me about how to
handle being president

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:03.130
was, don't eat dessert.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:05.962
[LAUGHING]

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.680
I believe the most
important thing

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.250
Paul taught by his
example was how

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:19.000
to succeed at creating change.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:24.110
At a place like MIT, with
such a legacy of excellence,

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:27.950
persuading people that
things need to change it's

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:32.340
a non-trivial exercise.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.570
But Paul understood
this community so well.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:41.680
He knew how to lead MIT to
live up to its full potential.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:45.960
And he also had the patience
for a lot of meetings.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.470
As a result, more than
anyone I can think of,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:54.855
Paul shaped the
MIT we know today.

00:07:54.855 --> 00:07:58.880
When you think of the programs
and progress he helped create,

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:02.750
it's impossible to
imagine MIT without them.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.990
Where would we be without
our undergraduate research

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:09.700
[INAUDIBLE] program, or Europe?

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.420
How did first year students ever
manage without pass [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.240
record.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.960
Would MIT even be MIT if we
did not welcome talented people

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.630
from every background,
and did not turn

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:26.610
outward to engage the world?

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.910
Each of these developments
represented at the time,

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:33.720
serious, startling change.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:38.159
But Paul helped our community
to see that each of these steps

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:41.700
was the bright open
path to possibility.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:48.610
I close with one final
Paul Gray lesson.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.430
As a white male who arrived
when the Institute itself

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.900
was overwhelmingly
white and male,

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.550
Paul was not an
obvious candidate

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.340
to lead MIT into a
new era of diversity.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.800
But as we will hear
today, he certainly did.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:13.480
And through that work, he proved
something extremely important,

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.560
that in the life
of our community,

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:23.960
cultural change and moral growth
are possible and imperative.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:27.950
In this complex, confusing
time for our nation,

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:31.550
and therefore for our
community, may Paul

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.680
be as he always was, our
guide to inventing a better

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.255
future for all.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.980
Good afternoon.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:07.640
I'm Shirley Anne Jackson, the
18th president of Rensselaer

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.920
Polytechnic Institute.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.530
a member of the MIT
classes of 1968 and 1973,

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.940
and a life member of
the MIT Corporation.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:23.120
First, I want to thank
Paul's wife, Priscilla, all

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.720
of his family, and
president Rafael

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.990
Reif for inviting me to
join this celebration

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:33.340
of the life of Paul Gray today.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:38.980
I admired and loved Paul
Gray, as did all of us.

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:43.060
He was someone I turn to
throughout my career, whenever

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.180
I had a difficult
decision to make,

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:51.330
because I knew that he would
guide me towards the right end.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:55.260
But beyond that, I've always
felt an enormous sense

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.000
of kinship with Paul because
we shared important moments

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.270
of leadership together.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:06.070
When I was an
undergraduate at MIT,

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.180
I was one of just two
African-American women

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.200
in my class.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.760
It was a lonely,
chilly experience.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.380
And although I was
a good student,

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.030
I was not entirely
accepted by my classmates,

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:25.230
or even by some
of my professors.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:29.580
In the spring of
1968, I was deciding

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:30.990
among graduate schools.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:33.780
And I was on the
way to the airport

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.770
after a visit to the
University of Pennsylvania

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.140
when I learned that the
rev. Dr. Martin Luther King

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:45.580
Jr. had been shot and killed
in Memphis, Tennessee.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.670
Inspired by the courage
and sacrifice of Dr. King

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:51.370
and others in the
civil rights movement,

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:56.350
I realized that MIT needed to
become a more welcoming place

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.570
for African-Americans,
for women,

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:03.070
for people from all
backgrounds and origins.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.580
And given that it's outsized
influence in science

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:12.290
and engineering, when you change
MIT, you change the world.

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:15.860
Now at the time, there were very
few African-American faculty

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:20.690
members or administrators
to spearhead such an effort.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:23.290
So a group of like-minded
students and I

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:26.020
formed the Black Students Union.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.050
And we presented
a list of demands

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:34.831
to the MIT administration,
only we called them proposals.

00:12:34.831 --> 00:12:37.780
[LAUGHING]

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:41.030
Now in response, MIT
formed the task force

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:46.170
on educational opportunity to
address the embedded issues.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:50.520
In its infinite wisdom,
MIT asked Paul Gray,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:53.190
who soon became
associate provost,

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.920
to take charge of the response.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.790
You know, Paul always said that
this was his first real chance

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:03.700
to exercise leadership
of any form at MIT.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.500
And that's a quote.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.690
And he quickly demonstrated that
he was a true leader, exactly

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:14.970
the right, mature person
for a turbulent time.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:19.840
He took what could have been
an adversarial situation,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:24.300
and sometimes it was, and
instantly identified our shared

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.070
objectives.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:29.380
And although he was MIT
through and through,

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:35.120
he immediately grasped that
MIT could and should be better.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:37.160
I was invited to
join the task force

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:38.960
on educational
opportunity, which

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.130
I did during my first year at
graduate school here at MIT.

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.250
I had decided that
I needed to remain

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:49.040
at MIT for graduate
school because I

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.970
knew that it was important to
work to change things here.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:56.140
And I felt that I could
make a difference.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:01.660
Now until that moment, so
Paul tells me, he had told me,

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.630
I feel like he's still
here, Paul had never

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.300
had the opportunity
before to get

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.480
to know any African-Americans,
having grown up

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:14.710
in an all white community, gone
to an all white high school,

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:18.700
and served in an all
white unit of the Army.

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:22.000
But importantly, he was
an empathetic person,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.620
who could always pull something
up from inside himself

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:32.070
to find common ground with the
person sitting across from him.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:36.330
As a teenager with an intense
interest in amateur radio

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:41.270
and electronics, he had
felt like an outsider.

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:44.290
He had some insight,
therefore, into how difficult

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:45.970
that could be.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:50.550
So he was ready to listen,
to learn, and to act.

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:54.470
Thanks to Paul, the task force
on educational opportunity

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:58.190
spurred a breathtaking
change at MIT.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:15:02.030
Until then, MIT had not done
much recruiting of any kind

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:03.920
for students, but why would it?

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:06.840
It was MIT, after all.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:10.410
But under Paul's leadership,
MIT hired an assistant director

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:13.710
of admissions who
was African-American.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.450
For the first
time, the Institute

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:19.050
developed special recruiting
materials and a stronger

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:22.860
financial aid package and
began visiting predominantly

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:25.530
African-American
high schools, saying

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:29.070
to the most talented
young men and women there,

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.160
we want you at MIT.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:36.330
Paul also had the wisdom to
send the Black Student Union

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:40.500
students, including me, on as
many of these recruiting trips

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.530
as we could manage
amidst our studies,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.960
to prove to African-American
high schoolers

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:50.940
that MIT was, indeed, a
realistic possibility.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:54.540
We had convinced Paul
that merely bringing

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:56.580
under-represented
minorities to MIT

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:59.550
was not enough, because
some of the high schools

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.950
did not offer advanced
science and mathematics.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:04.860
So they would be
starting in what

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:09.180
I had called an Olympic level
race from meters behind.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:13.380
They needed an introduction to
the rigorous course work of MIT

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:16.770
and to become confident that
they could succeed here.

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:20.700
So the task force also
initiated Project Interphase,

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:22.860
which continues to this day.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:26.640
Project Interphase was an
intense six week summer program

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.760
for incoming freshmen
who needed it,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.700
with classes in physics
and precalculus,

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.360
among other subjects, and
a wonderful introduction

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.520
to the MIT culture.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.170
I taught physics at Interphase.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:42.370
And although I was still
a graduate student,

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:47.340
I was asked to run the physics
program in its second year.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.860
As you can imagine, these
were radical changes

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:53.100
for MIT in the late 1960s.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:57.180
Paul was criticized, initially,
by some faculty members

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.690
who thought that he was lowering
the quality of the student

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:01.830
body.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:06.329
And some of my peers called
me a collaborationist

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:10.680
for working so closely with
the white administration.

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:15.900
Now Paul was even addressed in
a derogatory and profane fashion

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.329
by some very angry
African-American students

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:21.240
on one occasion.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:27.210
But Paul was always cool-headed,
even in the most heated times.

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:31.230
And he was tough when
he knew he was right.

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:34.080
The students Paul
helped to bring to MIT

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:37.560
thrived and proved
not just to MIT

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.680
but to the nation that
brilliance in science

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.200
and engineering is
not the province

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:48.300
of any single background,
ethnicity, or place of origin.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.850
That experience of
working with Paul

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:54.870
taught me, first,
what leadership is.

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.570
He also taught me
that I was a leader.

00:17:57.570 --> 00:18:00.750
And it led to many other
opportunities for me

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.570
throughout my life
and career, for which

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:05.940
I am immensely grateful.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:11.250
Paul also taught me by example
that leadership requires

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:15.990
toughness, tenacity, and
importantly a willingness

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.180
to be a bit of a
collaborationist.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.270
In other words, to
listen to others,

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:24.600
to identify points
of intersection,

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:29.410
and to work to turn
opponents into supporters.

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:32.140
Paul, as all of you know,
went on to become chancellor,

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:35.410
creating the first
formal plan to increase

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:37.990
the presence of
women and minorities

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:41.140
among the faculty, as
well as the student body.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:44.710
He then became the
14th president of MIT,

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:49.180
and later the chair of
the MIT Corporation.

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:52.840
And as you have heard, Paul
changed the MIT experience

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.850
in many important ways
throughout his career,

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.540
including modernizing the
electrical engineering

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:01.450
curriculum for the
age of semiconductors

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:07.210
in the early 1960s, and
championing a radical idea

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:11.740
that President Reif referenced,
the Undergraduate Research

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:16.180
Opportunities Program in
the late 1960s at the behest

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:21.130
of and in collaboration with
Margaret MacVicar, who also

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:25.210
happened to be my physics
tutor early in my career

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:27.200
in the women's dorm.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.480
Importantly, Paul and
Priscilla, as partners,

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:34.890
worked to make MIT a
more welcoming place

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:39.700
in myriad ways for everyone
here, especially the students.

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:43.960
Paul always cited increasing
the diversity of MIT

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:46.990
as his most important
accomplishment.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.680
And as you know, when he
arrived as an undergraduate,

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:52.860
fewer than one in
50 MIT students

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:55.610
was a woman or an
under-represented minority.

00:19:55.610 --> 00:20:00.310
And by the end of his
tenure as president in 1990,

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:04.180
women were more than 30%
of the incoming classes,

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:08.390
and underrepresented
minorities, 14%.

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:10.520
Now, Paul truly
changed the world

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:13.640
as an educational
innovator and by paving

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:16.400
the way for the rest
of us who continue

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:20.150
to work to bring the full
complement of young talent

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.400
into science and engineering.

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:25.910
But it was not just the
institutional Paul Gray,

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:31.080
but the personal Paul Gray who
was so important in my life.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:36.350
He was a sounding board,
mentor, supporter, and friend

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:38.330
throughout my career.

00:20:38.330 --> 00:20:41.870
We often talked about the
importance of education

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:44.600
for our national
well-being, especially

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:49.070
in science and engineering, and
about pedagogical innovation.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:53.300
Importantly, at crucial points
of decision and opportunity

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:57.170
in my life, I could
always turn to Paul,

00:20:57.170 --> 00:21:00.980
and did for advice and counsel.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:05.780
Moreover, he was my greatest
direct and personal advocate

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:08.390
for important
positions, including

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:12.290
the chairmanship of the US
Nuclear Regulatory Commission

00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:15.170
and the presidency of Rensselaer
Polytechnic Institute.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:19.100
In fact, when I was
developing the Rensselaer

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:24.500
plan, our strategic blueprint,
Paul came to the university

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:27.650
to help to explain to
our board of trustees

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.830
the crucial linkage of
research and teaching

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:34.430
in educating young people
in science and engineering.

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:36.140
And because of that,
it was a great honor

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:40.310
to have Paul write the
forward to a book that

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:43.880
was a retrospective of my
first 10 years as president

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.090
of Rensselaer.

00:21:46.090 --> 00:21:50.050
So it was a great privilege to
have shared a particular period

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:53.680
in history with Paul
Gray, and to have made

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:56.530
something happen alongside him.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:22:00.770
And later on, my own with
his support and friendship.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:04.840
It was a privilege, in fact,
just to have known him.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:07.010
Paul was kind.

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:09.010
He was sensible.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:11.200
He had moral courage.

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.110
And he grew into a dear friend.

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.780
And I will always
cherish the long talks

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:20.950
and walks we had when
my husband Morris and I

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:24.460
would visit Paul and Priscilla
in Rhode Island, often

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.020
in the winter.

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:28.450
And our hunts for
the crustaceans

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.880
that Priscilla loved so much.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:36.280
I'm so grateful to Paul Gray
for teaching me so much,

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:39.810
and for his incredible grace.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.840
I will miss him for
the rest of my life.

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:46.740
I thank all of you for allowing
me to honor this great man.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.050
Priscilla and members
of the Gray family,

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:16.420
Rafael and members
of the MIT community,

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:20.050
distinguished guests, ladies
and gentlemen, I'm Victor Fung.

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:21.910
I'm from Hong Kong.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:25.696
I am from MIT class of '66.

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:35.330
And Paul Gray was my thesis
advisor for my master's thesis.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.300
I can't tell you what a
great honor and privilege

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:42.920
it is for me to speak at this
service for an MIT legend

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:46.670
whose life and humanity
touched all of us.

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.980
Paul Gray's belief in
diversity and inclusion,

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:51.810
as borne out by
Shirley Jackson's

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:55.620
brilliant achievements,
also benefited me

00:23:55.620 --> 00:24:00.100
when I was a 20-year-old
MIT student from Hong Kong.

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:03.840
Indeed, some 50 years
ago, Paul wrote me

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.390
a letter that has
since been a guiding

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.720
light for my entire career.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:14.100
It was 1966 and I was doing
a thesis for my master's

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.330
in electrical engineering.

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:19.440
In fact, I was blessed
to be the last thesis

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:23.310
student Professor Gray
mentored before going

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:26.370
full time into administration.

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:29.230
It was most gracious
of him to take me on,

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:33.420
as he was already extremely busy
as associate dean for Student

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.580
Affairs.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:39.270
Paul set aside an hour every
week out of his very busy

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.040
schedule-- and if my
memory serves me correctly,

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:45.180
it was 4 PM every Thursday--

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:48.990
for me to come to his office so
that he could guide and review

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:50.410
my progress.

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:54.360
I still remember his office
was on the fourth door

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.700
on the beginning of
the Infinite Corridor,

00:24:56.700 --> 00:25:02.700
as you go in on 77 Mass Ave. I
must confess, I was not always

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:07.870
punctual, nor was my
attendance entirely reliable.

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:10.800
I think I might have
missed one or two times.

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:15.630
I was doing a co-op program at
General Radio during the day

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.570
and then working on
my thesis at night.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:21.930
But keeping a dean waiting
was not the smartest thing

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:25.200
that I've ever done.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.770
The thesis was about
computer-aided design

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:31.380
of Schmitt trigger
circuits, very technical.

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:33.720
In fact, I read the
thesis again recently

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.040
and I couldn't believe
that was what I wrote.

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:44.930
When I finished, Paul wrote
me a five page letter.

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:48.740
The gist was, based on
the quality and content

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:52.580
of the work in your thesis,
I'm giving you an A.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:57.620
But I want you to know that I'm
giving you this A reluctantly.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:26:00.230
The way you plan and
structure your work

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:07.240
and your interaction with me
do not deserve an A. [LAUGHING]

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:13.182
Of course, I told my parents in
Hong Kong only about the grade.

00:26:13.182 --> 00:26:14.890
I didn't dare tell
them about the letter.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:21.870
But as I started my career,
Paul's treasured letter

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:24.160
gave me much food for thought.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:28.800
I have reread it many,
many times over the years.

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:32.450
Paul really impressed upon me
that having the bright idea

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:34.726
is not enough.

00:26:34.726 --> 00:26:36.100
You've got to have
the discipline

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:38.470
to put those ideas
into a context

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.870
where they can be effective.

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:44.080
If you wish to end up with a
final piece or a final product

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:49.240
that is truly excellent, you
have got to execute properly.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.730
I'm sure each and
every one of you

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:55.630
here today have been touched
by this wonderful individual

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:58.180
at some point in
your careers and

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.750
in his own very special way.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:06.560
That is not all that
I've learned from Paul.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:10.580
In him, I found a man whose
values resonate deeply

00:27:10.580 --> 00:27:16.550
with those of my own family,
commitment, loyalty, fairness,

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:19.600
and I have just
indicated frankness.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:24.500
Paul also cared passionately
that technological change

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:28.460
should be a force for good
in communities and societies

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:29.810
globally.

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:33.830
And ethos, which continues to
inspire and drive our family

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:36.500
company, Lee and Fung, today.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:39.710
Long ago, on one of
Paul's many visits

00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:42.530
to Hong Kong as
MIT president, he

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.440
asked if I would consider
making a meaningful donation

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:47.820
to this Institute.

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:50.250
I explained that I couldn't
at the time because I

00:27:50.250 --> 00:27:53.550
was putting every cent I had
into a buyout of the family

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:55.300
company.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.510
But I made a commitment that
when I did have the means,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.700
I would be back.

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:04.630
Imagine, then, what a joy it
was to see Paul and Priscilla,

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:08.020
at last year's ceremony,
announcing MIT's new

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:11.350
Make a Space at the
Metropolitan Warehouse,

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:15.820
which my brother William and
I are honored to support.

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:18.830
It was the last time I saw Paul.

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:22.580
I so wanted Paul to know that
the seed he planted years ago

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:26.420
had ripened, for reasons that
include my lifelong gratitude

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:29.960
to him and to MIT.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:34.670
When he became my mentor,
Paul was only 34 years old,

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:37.640
younger than my
children are today.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:40.730
Yet the serious,
proper man always

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:43.940
seemed wiser than
his years, just as he

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:47.410
was years ahead of his time.

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:49.850
Paul was truly the
master who appeared

00:28:49.850 --> 00:28:52.070
when a student was ready.

00:28:52.070 --> 00:28:54.560
He helped me formulate
my life and what

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:57.080
to make of it, for sure.

00:28:57.080 --> 00:29:00.140
I shall be keeping that
letter of his safely

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:04.940
under lock and key for future
generations of my family.

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:05.440
Thank you.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:33.860
Good afternoon.

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:35.980
My name is Jim Taylor.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:38.860
I sometimes respond
to the description

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:41.840
of the younger brother
that Paul never had

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:44.110
and the older brother
that Virginia never had.

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:51.280
In 1965, I was a senior at
MIT and looking for a way

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:55.900
to finance graduate school
and avoid the Vietnam War.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:59.710
Providentially, Ken Wadley,
then dean of student affairs,

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:03.910
was bringing on board a new
associate dean and needed

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:08.050
a grunt to do some
work with respect

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:10.840
to the freshmen curriculum.

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:13.690
Paul Gray, an
untenured professor

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:17.470
of electrical engineering,
became that associate dean

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:20.290
and I was the grunt.

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:22.300
Between us, over
the next 18 years,

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:25.420
we did some-- over
the next 18 months,

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:28.780
we did some work around
the freshman program

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.270
which resulted in some changes.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:34.450
But more importantly,
we established

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:38.740
a lifelong friendship that
can never be replaced.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:42.010
Paul became my role model.

00:30:42.010 --> 00:30:43.672
Now, role model can
take many forms.

00:30:43.672 --> 00:30:45.130
And I've come to
believe that there

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:47.990
are at least four elements
to being a role model.

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:53.430
I call them the four F's,
faith, fitness, family,

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:56.224
and profession.

00:30:56.224 --> 00:31:01.570
[LAUGHING] And the
search for somebody

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:03.550
that embodies all of
those is very difficult.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:04.910
Many people never find that.

00:31:04.910 --> 00:31:09.120
But I was fortunate enough to
both find it and recognize it

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.770
early on.

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:13.200
So Paul became the
standard by which

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:17.430
I have measured much of
the remainder of my life.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:20.540
I won't to say a lot about
the professional element.

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:25.150
Paul's 47 years at MIT, what
he called the special place,

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:27.600
has been well documented
and will be well described

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:30.850
by other people.

00:31:30.850 --> 00:31:33.617
But I would like to just talk
quickly about the project

00:31:33.617 --> 00:31:35.700
that we worked on together
because it was actually

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:39.450
the first thing that Paul did
in an administrative capacity

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:42.540
to benefit MIT.

00:31:42.540 --> 00:31:45.870
Through the mid '60s,
the freshmen curriculum

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:49.620
was absolutely identical
for all freshman.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:53.160
Chemistry, physics,
math, humanities,

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:56.160
absolutely the same courses
taught in the same way,

00:31:56.160 --> 00:31:58.605
regardless of the high
school student preparation

00:31:58.605 --> 00:32:01.452
or regardless of
academic intention.

00:32:01.452 --> 00:32:02.910
And Paul always
asked the question,

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.530
is that the way it ought to be?

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:08.700
So we did some studies on that.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:10.770
We had some hypotheses to test.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:14.160
For example, I call these
intuitively obvious.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.180
I'm not sure why we
had to analyze them,

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:20.820
but for example, if a student
in high school had had calculus,

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:23.880
would they do better
in calculus and physics

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:27.780
at MIT than if they
hadn't had calculus.

00:32:27.780 --> 00:32:29.460
That seemed pretty
obvious, but we

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.220
needed to analyze that, bring
some data to bear on it,

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.140
come up with some conclusions.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:38.730
So we had a lot of data
on freshman classes

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:41.110
and we had the state
of the art computing.

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:44.040
Now, state of the
art computing in 1965

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:48.480
was an IBM 1402 and an IBM 84.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:51.450
It's unlikely that most of you
know what those numbers mean,

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:55.035
but it's actually a punch
card reader and a card sorter.

00:32:57.940 --> 00:33:02.040
That is what we used
for computing support.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:05.130
Needless to say,
our analytical work

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.800
verified that there should be
some changes in the freshman

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:10.740
program, that it needn't
be monolithic and totally

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.120
structured the same
way for all students.

00:33:13.120 --> 00:33:16.620
And as a result of that, MIT
implemented some variations

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:20.880
in chemistry, physics,
especially physics and math,

00:33:20.880 --> 00:33:23.250
and permitted students
to take variations

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:28.730
depending on their academic
interests and future.

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:32.090
What I learned from
that was not only

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:34.220
what I would call the
obvious conclusions,

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:35.720
but more importantly,
the way Paul

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:38.210
approached complex problems.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:40.640
His clarity of thought
was incredible.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:42.440
His integrity,
his mutual respect

00:33:42.440 --> 00:33:45.200
for others, including
his grunt, just

00:33:45.200 --> 00:33:48.050
came through in that
project and set standards

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:52.160
for my own professional career.

00:33:52.160 --> 00:33:54.620
I'll mention the
faith element briefly.

00:33:54.620 --> 00:33:57.150
Paul didn't talk
much about his faith.

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:59.210
He simply lived it.

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:02.570
He was a longtime member at
Hancock Church in Lexington

00:34:02.570 --> 00:34:04.850
where Priscilla still worships.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.620
They were active in
a small group Bible

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:08.840
study for more than 50 years.

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:11.090
They traveled together,
socialized, but mostly

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:12.860
studied the Bible.

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:16.850
I marveled at how he served in
key positions in key committees

00:34:16.850 --> 00:34:20.480
at his church while fulfilling
demanding jobs at MIT.

00:34:23.260 --> 00:34:27.580
Fitness, and its
companion, food,

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:31.810
represented a wide area for
Paul and me to explore together.

00:34:31.810 --> 00:34:34.280
We played squash intensely.

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:36.370
And I learned not to
take control of the t

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:39.040
when Paul was behind me.

00:34:39.040 --> 00:34:44.290
I learned the significance
of the phrase, coming around.

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:47.020
Paul taught me to
drink my coffee black,

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:48.760
how to enjoy butter
in every nook

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.860
and cranny of English muffins.

00:34:51.860 --> 00:34:55.630
We love Priscilla's
lobster boils, lasagna,

00:34:55.630 --> 00:34:57.460
and her sticky buns.

00:34:57.460 --> 00:35:01.090
We shared Thanksgiving dinners
in the president's house.

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:08.320
We ate Tex-Mex in Corpus Christi
where our son, Paul's namesake,

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:12.070
was born more than 40 years ago.

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:16.120
We suffered the same sweet
tooth for cookies and ice cream.

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:19.090
When I took up
running, Paul tried

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:21.250
to convince me
that rice at Royal

00:35:21.250 --> 00:35:25.145
east was an acceptable form of
carb loading before the Boston

00:35:25.145 --> 00:35:25.645
Marathon.

00:35:28.820 --> 00:35:30.033
He was wrong.

00:35:30.033 --> 00:35:33.820
[LAUGHING]

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:36.040
In our early days, we
hiked the White Mountains

00:35:36.040 --> 00:35:37.840
with Virginia and Amy.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.190
In recent years, we walked
slowly around Walden Pond.

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:44.980
But for me, Paul's
most significant impact

00:35:44.980 --> 00:35:48.670
was through his
dedication to his family.

00:35:48.670 --> 00:35:51.970
When you got to know Paul,
you got the whole family.

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:55.120
His parents set him off from
Livingston New Jersey to MIT

00:35:55.120 --> 00:35:59.230
and were always a bit puzzled
as to why he never left.

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:01.300
Shortly after I started
working for Paul,

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:04.990
I met Priscilla, his
partner in all things.

00:36:04.990 --> 00:36:07.390
Virginia was a
precocious eight-year-old

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:09.130
pushing the boundaries.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:11.980
Amy was a fine arts
talent, especially creative

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:14.230
in fabric and textiles.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:17.560
Andrew was Paul's buddy, trying
out new things, including

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:19.890
each other's patience.

00:36:19.890 --> 00:36:22.900
And Louise was then,
and now, the pixie,

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:25.080
the center of impish fun.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:28.300
Our lives have
intertwined ever since.

00:36:28.300 --> 00:36:30.310
As I witness family
life for the Grays,

00:36:30.310 --> 00:36:32.710
it followed a few
simple principles.

00:36:32.710 --> 00:36:36.680
Spend time together,
be serious, have fun,

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.520
be deliberate about
creating memories.

00:36:39.520 --> 00:36:41.290
Celebrate together.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:42.790
Grieve together.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:44.952
Never compromise your integrity.

00:36:47.850 --> 00:36:51.160
Easy to say, hard to
put into practice.

00:36:51.160 --> 00:36:53.060
And it does sound
nearly perfect.

00:36:53.060 --> 00:36:56.410
While I admit I had to do a
little bit of fine tuning.

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:59.760
I had to rescue his children
from the monotony of Mozart

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:02.520
by introducing them to Herb
Alpert and the Tijuana Brass.

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:09.080
You'll hear a lot about
Paul's legacy today.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:11.150
But for me, it's
pretty personal.

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:14.810
It sustains me in the areas
of faith, fitness, family,

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:17.040
and profession.

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:19.260
I'd like to think that I'm
a better person for having

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:21.869
traveled in Paul's wake.

00:37:21.869 --> 00:37:23.910
And I hope that's been
passed down to some degree

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:26.310
to my children
and grandchildren.

00:37:26.310 --> 00:37:28.350
But multiply that
by thousands, I

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:30.870
daresay nearly everybody here.

00:37:30.870 --> 00:37:34.380
We've all woven a thread of
Paul Gray into our lives.

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:35.580
That's his lasting legacy.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:54.020
A year ago, in one of my visits
to see Priscilla and Paul,

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:56.240
he wanted to make
a special breakfast

00:37:56.240 --> 00:37:59.240
on the day I was
leaving to return home.

00:37:59.240 --> 00:38:01.630
He asked the night
before what I'd like.

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:03.230
And I said, I really
liked the omelets

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:07.010
he made in Little Compton, with
Swiss cheese and fresh chives.

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:09.320
That struck him as doable.

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:11.210
So the next morning
after about an hour

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:13.550
of scurrying around
in the kitchen,

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:18.278
he proudly presented breakfast,
delicious blueberry pancakes.

00:38:18.278 --> 00:38:24.170
[LAUGHING] It was such
a touching moment,

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:27.560
equal parts sweet and said.

00:38:27.560 --> 00:38:28.370
I miss Paul.

00:38:34.358 --> 00:38:39.348
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:41:57.580 --> 00:42:03.310
It was my privilege 26 years
ago to award you your bachelor's

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:09.100
degree, and 25 years ago,
your master's degree.

00:42:09.100 --> 00:42:12.340
It was soon recognized that
your first rate performance

00:42:12.340 --> 00:42:16.000
as a teacher was
matched by first rate

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:19.240
talent as an administrator.

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:23.200
And you were quickly co-opted
from one administrative job

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:25.000
after another.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:27.970
In fact, you hardly
had the opportunity

00:42:27.970 --> 00:42:31.060
to arm the chair in
one post before you

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:32.561
were drafted for another.

00:42:32.561 --> 00:42:36.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:42:43.150 --> 00:42:45.850
It's very nice to
have so many people

00:42:45.850 --> 00:42:48.940
from so many institutions and
so many old friends who've

00:42:48.940 --> 00:42:51.554
come back today to participate
in this celebration.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:57.670
I got to know Paul Gray when I
was beginning graduate school.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:01.840
But we really became close
when I became his provost.

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:04.900
I first met him through
his deep involvement

00:43:04.900 --> 00:43:08.210
in undergraduate
education at MIT.

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:12.130
He was a great friend,
a great colleague,

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.620
but also a very
great teacher for me.

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:22.710
I got involved as an athlete,
as a swimmer, as a rower,

00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:23.580
as a sailor.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:26.100
And I would often see him
and I would invite him

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:27.720
to our competitions.

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:28.890
And he would come.

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:32.480
And I remember he and Priscilla
coming in to a swimming meet

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:35.880
and just sitting down, and
staying for the whole time.

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:39.660
He was a quietly very
brave and tenacious man,

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:44.580
loving, and kind, and
gentle, and very focused,

00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:47.790
and always very engaged.

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:52.650
He loved being around experts,
and taking advice from experts,

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:57.060
and learning from experts,
and sharing with experts.

00:43:57.060 --> 00:44:02.680
He was the father who took
us hiking, biking, swimming,

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:05.362
sailing, skating.

00:44:05.362 --> 00:44:06.820
He's the one who
ran behind us when

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:09.111
we were learning to ride our
bikes until we could do it

00:44:09.111 --> 00:44:09.924
on our own.

00:44:09.924 --> 00:44:11.340
And there were no
training wheels,

00:44:11.340 --> 00:44:12.340
you just figured it out.

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:20.184
Paul began his MIT
life as a freshman.

00:44:20.184 --> 00:44:23.630
Paul came to MIT in the 1950s,
so that's almost 70 years.

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:25.150
That's just simply remarkable.

00:44:25.150 --> 00:44:28.570
I cannot think of anybody else
who gave so much of his life

00:44:28.570 --> 00:44:31.720
and shared so much of his
life with this institute.

00:44:31.720 --> 00:44:34.210
He's a very down to
earth individual,

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:38.000
very straightforward
man, but very caring.

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:39.110
He did so much for MIT.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:40.930
And he did it loyally.

00:44:40.930 --> 00:44:44.010
He did it morning,
noon, and night.

00:44:44.010 --> 00:44:47.520
He was so devoted to this
place and to the students

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:49.890
in such a genuine way.

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:52.090
He was never in it for himself.

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:54.200
He was always in it for MIT.

00:44:57.350 --> 00:45:01.580
Some year ago, in a
commencement in the rain,

00:45:01.580 --> 00:45:05.060
a father was heard to
say, after the soaking

00:45:05.060 --> 00:45:07.690
I've taken for the last four
years, who minds a little rain?

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:14.761
But I have to say,
this is something else.

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:23.040
In the competitive
sport of diving,

00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:25.470
the various dives
are ranked according

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:28.670
to the degree of difficulty.

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:32.610
When the performances of
different divers are equal,

00:45:32.610 --> 00:45:34.890
the diver who chooses
the dive with the highest

00:45:34.890 --> 00:45:38.070
degree of difficulty wins.

00:45:38.070 --> 00:45:40.920
Now by that measure alone,
you here today are winners.

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:47.390
You have chosen and
successfully negotiated

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:51.050
a collegiate education having
what may be the nation's

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:55.000
highest degree of difficulty.

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:57.580
You know, he always call
it this special place.

00:45:57.580 --> 00:46:00.190
And I think for him it
really was a special place

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:03.460
and a kind of home unlike
he'd ever had before,

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:06.190
because it satisfied
him intellectually

00:46:06.190 --> 00:46:08.860
and it satisfied him,
I think, emotionally.

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:11.800
And when he was able to
connect his own family to it,

00:46:11.800 --> 00:46:15.410
then I think that that
was sort of a full circle.

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:17.845
MIT is best known for the
scientists and engineers

00:46:17.845 --> 00:46:19.300
who have studied there.

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:24.730
He was at MIT at a time when
engineering changed completely

00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:28.690
from a largely empirical
practice of making

00:46:28.690 --> 00:46:31.720
things and making them
work, to a situation

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:35.350
where science and understanding
fundamentals effected

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:37.930
the design and the
implementation of so

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:40.030
many systems.

00:46:40.030 --> 00:46:47.500
This very gentle, quiet, fierce
man, I know had to do battle

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:49.990
to get Whitehead
Institute established.

00:46:49.990 --> 00:46:52.510
People were terribly suspicious.

00:46:52.510 --> 00:46:55.030
And you know, frankly, he
wasn't sure what it would be.

00:46:55.030 --> 00:46:57.130
Nobody was.

00:46:57.130 --> 00:46:58.975
It was an experiment
and he took the risk.

00:47:03.320 --> 00:47:06.830
MIT and the biomedical
enterprise and Kendall Square

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:09.150
is unrivaled anywhere
in the world.

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:11.750
And so I do credit Paul
with setting us out

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:12.560
in that direction.

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:15.950
So at the Whitehead, we now
have The Broad, and the Reagan,

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:19.070
the Koch Institute, any
number of expressions

00:47:19.070 --> 00:47:25.390
of the power of biology
to transform our world.

00:47:25.390 --> 00:47:31.480
When I came to MIT, which was
in 1965, I think maybe 2% or 3%

00:47:31.480 --> 00:47:35.710
of the students were women,
fewer than that on the faculty.

00:47:35.710 --> 00:47:39.280
It was very much a
male institution.

00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:42.570
He was tremendously
committed to diversity,

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:46.540
increasing the representation
in the faculty and the student

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:50.160
body of women and of minorities.

00:47:50.160 --> 00:47:53.490
The number of entering
African-American students

00:47:53.490 --> 00:48:00.650
at MIT went from about
3 to 5 per year, to 57.

00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:03.920
To this day, I mean, we're just
very proud of that momentum

00:48:03.920 --> 00:48:07.020
that Paul brought to the
table in this [INAUDIBLE]..

00:48:07.020 --> 00:48:12.210
MIT has a responsibility to
itself and to the nation,

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:16.230
to be open, and
indeed to reach out

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:20.700
to the most talented and
promising people, regardless

00:48:20.700 --> 00:48:23.550
of race or sex.

00:48:23.550 --> 00:48:25.360
None of us created all
the bias around us,

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:27.270
but we certainly inherit it.

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:29.160
And what I love
about President Gray

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:31.420
is that he stepped
right into that.

00:48:31.420 --> 00:48:33.600
And as soon as he saw
it and understood it,

00:48:33.600 --> 00:48:36.865
he decided to actually
do something about it.

00:48:36.865 --> 00:48:40.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:44.290 --> 00:48:49.510
During his inauguration, what he
wanted as the walking in music

00:48:49.510 --> 00:48:53.920
was Aaron Copeland's,
Fanfare for the Common Man.

00:48:53.920 --> 00:48:55.510
And that was him, you know?

00:48:58.330 --> 00:49:01.300
He always was in touch with
his very modest beginnings

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:04.840
in a blue collar
family where, you know,

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:06.590
the economy was a challenge.

00:49:13.414 --> 00:49:15.080
One of his fraternity
brothers, while he

00:49:15.080 --> 00:49:17.810
was at MIT as a sophomore,
was dating a woman

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:20.354
at Wheaton College, where
my mother was a freshman.

00:49:20.354 --> 00:49:21.770
They introduced
my parents on what

00:49:21.770 --> 00:49:24.390
turned out to be a rather
successful blind date.

00:49:24.390 --> 00:49:26.570
It was very cold.

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:30.140
And they're walking
across the bridge.

00:49:30.140 --> 00:49:33.850
And my mother doesn't
have any gloves.

00:49:33.850 --> 00:49:38.830
And my father said, I've
got a pair of gloves.

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:40.420
Why don't we do this?

00:49:40.420 --> 00:49:44.060
I'll wear the left one,
you wear the right one,

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:46.960
and we'll hold hands to
keep the middle ones warm.

00:49:46.960 --> 00:49:49.730
And they walked
across the bridge.

00:49:49.730 --> 00:49:51.280
And they walked off
into the future.

00:49:51.280 --> 00:49:54.730
That was the first day
of their relationship.

00:49:54.730 --> 00:49:58.030
I thought, well, that's pretty
good for a nerd from New

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:00.390
Jersey.

00:50:00.390 --> 00:50:04.545
A very clever use of a pair of
gloves to win the girl's heart.

00:50:13.960 --> 00:50:17.960
It was the big blizzard
of '78 and no cars

00:50:17.960 --> 00:50:20.420
were allowed on the
road for a week.

00:50:20.420 --> 00:50:23.100
But MIT was not
going to shut down.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:25.940
So we started this bus line.

00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:28.400
And one of my favorite
pictures of Paul Gray

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:33.350
is with his big wolf
skin or beaver skin hat,

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:38.270
you know, like a Russian hat,
out in the parking lot at MIT

00:50:38.270 --> 00:50:40.850
where the buses came
in directing traffic.

00:50:40.850 --> 00:50:43.580
I mean, he was
very much hands on.

00:50:43.580 --> 00:50:46.610
He looks incredibly happy,
actually, in the picture.

00:50:46.610 --> 00:50:48.820
He was just doing what
he could do, I think,

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:50.570
and having fun doing it.

00:50:50.570 --> 00:50:54.500
I think he had a lot
of joy in what he did.

00:50:54.500 --> 00:50:57.530
He was a real proponent of
the Undergraduate Research

00:50:57.530 --> 00:51:00.140
Opportunities Program, UROP.

00:51:00.140 --> 00:51:02.660
It was dramatically
successful and found

00:51:02.660 --> 00:51:06.050
a way for undergraduates to
interact with the faculty

00:51:06.050 --> 00:51:08.220
in their research projects.

00:51:08.220 --> 00:51:10.010
This would be a way
in which students

00:51:10.010 --> 00:51:12.680
could, for the first time
perhaps in our years at MIT,

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:17.240
come to terms with an unsolved,
real problem, something

00:51:17.240 --> 00:51:20.740
where they could make
a genuine contribution.

00:51:20.740 --> 00:51:24.200
That became an integral part
of how MIT educates students,

00:51:24.200 --> 00:51:27.230
so it was a huge thing for us.

00:51:27.230 --> 00:51:30.200
When we finished up all the
administrative and leadership

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:33.210
roles that he had at MIT,
went back to the classroom.

00:51:33.210 --> 00:51:34.880
He loved being in the classroom.

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:37.220
You turn the crank
on a three by three

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:44.110
matrix and solve for the node
voltages, e sub b, e sub c,

00:51:44.110 --> 00:51:46.340
and e sub d.

00:51:46.340 --> 00:51:49.370
He was an absolutely
excellent teacher.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:54.230
Clarity, complete concern with
the student's understanding,

00:51:54.230 --> 00:51:57.860
very much enjoyed
being in their company.

00:51:57.860 --> 00:52:01.460
Professor Gray was
my academic advisor

00:52:01.460 --> 00:52:03.650
when I was an
undergrad here at MIT.

00:52:03.650 --> 00:52:06.095
And I remember he was a
really warm, very friendly

00:52:06.095 --> 00:52:08.884
and giving advisor.

00:52:08.884 --> 00:52:10.300
At first, maybe
you would think it

00:52:10.300 --> 00:52:13.360
might be a little intimidating
having your advisor be

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:14.744
the ex-president of MIT.

00:52:14.744 --> 00:52:16.660
But that turned out not
to be the case at all.

00:52:16.660 --> 00:52:20.350
Professor Gray was
extremely down to earth.

00:52:20.350 --> 00:52:23.920
He really understood
technological education

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:27.700
and what a technological
research university is really

00:52:27.700 --> 00:52:29.688
about, and how precious it is.

00:52:32.440 --> 00:52:34.820
One of the many involvements
that the Grays had

00:52:34.820 --> 00:52:38.420
with students were
these senior dinners.

00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:42.880
We invited the whole class to
dinner, 60 or 70 at a time.

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:47.470
All of us at MIT, we just
celebrate the opportunities

00:52:47.470 --> 00:52:48.880
to be with those students.

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:51.530
They are so special.

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:55.370
Every semester on Registration
Day, we would meet with him

00:52:55.370 --> 00:52:57.980
and then that evening all
of us, all of his advisees,

00:52:57.980 --> 00:53:00.770
would go out to dinner with
him, always at the Royal East.

00:53:00.770 --> 00:53:03.680
If you can imagine, there
was sort of a big table,

00:53:03.680 --> 00:53:07.030
usually in the back of the
restaurant, all filled with,

00:53:07.030 --> 00:53:10.580
I'd say, maybe about
10 or so advisees.

00:53:10.580 --> 00:53:15.950
Yours is the obligation to
help heal society where healing

00:53:15.950 --> 00:53:22.550
is required, to help strengthen
the nations where strengthening

00:53:22.550 --> 00:53:27.050
is required, and to
help bring harmony

00:53:27.050 --> 00:53:32.990
among the nations of this
world, wherever discord obtains.

00:53:32.990 --> 00:53:38.060
If you have ever seen Paul's
official presidential portrait,

00:53:38.060 --> 00:53:41.300
it is a portrait of
Paul and Priscilla.

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:43.940
He was the first person to
have his spouse in the picture,

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:44.930
right?

00:53:44.930 --> 00:53:46.610
And I think that
for him that was

00:53:46.610 --> 00:53:49.200
a measure of his
devotion for her,

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:50.840
his appreciation
for all the things

00:53:50.840 --> 00:53:53.480
that my mother may have
sacrificed in order to help

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:58.887
him have the roles and the
opportunities that he had,

00:53:58.887 --> 00:54:00.720
and all the ways in
which she supported him.

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:02.386
I think that that was
his way of saying,

00:54:02.386 --> 00:54:06.322
I wouldn't have been able to
do this without Priscilla.

00:54:06.322 --> 00:54:09.470
It was extraordinary
to watch them together,

00:54:09.470 --> 00:54:12.170
how they worked together, how
they supported each other.

00:54:12.170 --> 00:54:13.670
They both had a
twinkle in their eye

00:54:13.670 --> 00:54:16.980
that would kind of ping at you.

00:54:16.980 --> 00:54:19.590
Just to know that there was
someone sort of watching over

00:54:19.590 --> 00:54:21.900
us, making sure that things,
generally, are probably

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:22.900
going to be OK for us.

00:54:22.900 --> 00:54:26.790
So I really appreciated
that very much.

00:54:26.790 --> 00:54:31.130
He is a man of
exceptional integrity.

00:54:31.130 --> 00:54:35.570
The humanness of the
man and of Priscilla

00:54:35.570 --> 00:54:41.090
was so essential to the kind of
leadership he provided at MIT.

00:54:41.090 --> 00:54:42.996
He cared about people.

00:54:42.996 --> 00:54:46.275
A person with
tremendous integrity.

00:54:46.275 --> 00:54:53.990
A person with tremendous
wisdom, thoughtful, calm.

00:54:53.990 --> 00:54:57.950
Extraordinarily forceful
person, but also

00:54:57.950 --> 00:55:01.860
with a surprisingly
gentle personality.

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:03.210
He was good at listening.

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:06.810
He was good at negotiating
and meeting people part way.

00:55:06.810 --> 00:55:11.190
And he was good at imagining
what might be possible

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:14.520
if this shifted or that changed,
or we could move forward

00:55:14.520 --> 00:55:16.380
together in this direction.

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:19.680
As you undertake this
next stage in your lives,

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:22.770
I wish for you a life that
is rich in opportunities

00:55:22.770 --> 00:55:27.780
to stretch your talents, your
interests, your imagination,

00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:31.170
and your vision of
this small planet

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:35.500
as a beautiful oasis,
generating peace.

00:55:35.500 --> 00:55:37.710
And as you depart from
this special place,

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:40.136
I wish you God speed.

00:55:40.136 --> 00:55:44.582
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:03.280
Well, that was a remarkable,
remarkable tribute.

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:05.000
I'm Susan Hockfield.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:10.060
And I served as the
16th president of MIT.

00:56:10.060 --> 00:56:13.960
And it is a very
great but a very sad

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:17.440
privilege to offer a
few words in celebration

00:56:17.440 --> 00:56:23.620
of the 14th president
of MIT, Paul E. Gray.

00:56:23.620 --> 00:56:28.060
I first met Paul Gray during
my initial introduction at MIT.

00:56:28.060 --> 00:56:31.360
Paul served as a member
of the presidential search

00:56:31.360 --> 00:56:34.690
committee that brought
me to the Institute.

00:56:34.690 --> 00:56:37.930
Even among the truly impressive
and distinguished committee

00:56:37.930 --> 00:56:42.190
members, Paul, by any measure,
held the deepest knowledge

00:56:42.190 --> 00:56:45.430
and the greatest breadth
of understanding of MIT.

00:56:45.430 --> 00:56:51.340
However, even among that
group, with that task at hand,

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:54.310
he demonstrated one of
his core characteristics,

00:56:54.310 --> 00:56:58.460
doing far more
listening than speaking.

00:56:58.460 --> 00:57:00.940
In those interviews and
first conversations,

00:57:00.940 --> 00:57:05.110
I came to understand that
Paul's patience as a listener

00:57:05.110 --> 00:57:08.530
captured the essential
trait of the greatest

00:57:08.530 --> 00:57:13.420
teachers, a remarkable skill
in leading us, his students,

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:17.180
to discover the
answers ourselves.

00:57:17.180 --> 00:57:21.230
When I arrived on campus as
the Institute's newly minted

00:57:21.230 --> 00:57:24.920
president, Paul became my
first and most essential guide

00:57:24.920 --> 00:57:26.330
to MIT.

00:57:26.330 --> 00:57:30.350
His love of the place, of the
people, and of our mission,

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:34.740
shone brightly in all
he said and all he did.

00:57:34.740 --> 00:57:37.440
As my family joined
our new community,

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:40.740
he and his beloved
Priscilla warmly

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:43.740
adopted us, giving
Tom, Elizabeth, and me

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:48.020
a profoundly appreciated
sense of being at home.

00:57:48.020 --> 00:57:51.360
A part of me has
always and will always

00:57:51.360 --> 00:57:57.150
see MIT through Paul's eyes, and
through Priscilla's eyes too.

00:57:57.150 --> 00:58:00.600
The two of them,
inseparably, led MIT

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:04.530
in head, hand, and heart.

00:58:04.530 --> 00:58:06.510
Paul, as we've heard
again and again,

00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:10.740
transformed the Institute
in a host of important ways.

00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:14.040
Looking through a
particular set of lenses

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:17.790
one can see how it was he who
paved the path that brought me

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:22.650
to MIT, as the Institute's
first woman and first biologist

00:58:22.650 --> 00:58:25.380
to serve as president.

00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:30.190
As President Jackson has
described so beautifully,

00:58:30.190 --> 00:58:33.210
Paul considered his work
expanding opportunities

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:36.330
for women and minorities
to be his most important

00:58:36.330 --> 00:58:38.470
contribution.

00:58:38.470 --> 00:58:40.750
He understood
deeply that, for MIT

00:58:40.750 --> 00:58:43.870
to serve the nation and
the world most effectively,

00:58:43.870 --> 00:58:46.540
we needed to bring more of
the most talented people

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:51.190
toward campus, no matter
their gender or phenotype.

00:58:51.190 --> 00:58:54.550
He championed admitting more
women and minority students,

00:58:54.550 --> 00:58:57.790
and bringing greater diversity
to our faculty and staff,

00:58:57.790 --> 00:59:01.660
not just in words,
but also in deeds.

00:59:01.660 --> 00:59:04.720
The number of women
students and faculty grew.

00:59:04.720 --> 00:59:08.980
And he appointed women
to important roles.

00:59:08.980 --> 00:59:12.280
Margaret MacVicar's leadership
of the paradigm-breaking

00:59:12.280 --> 00:59:15.010
Undergraduate Research
Opportunities Program,

00:59:15.010 --> 00:59:18.490
UROP, transformed
a core tenant of

00:59:18.490 --> 00:59:21.280
the undergraduate experience.

00:59:21.280 --> 00:59:23.890
Through UROP, we
invite our students

00:59:23.890 --> 00:59:27.910
to move beyond receiving
knowledge in our classrooms,

00:59:27.910 --> 00:59:32.290
to discovering knowledge
in our laboratories.

00:59:32.290 --> 00:59:34.210
When Paul arrived
as an undergraduate,

00:59:34.210 --> 00:59:38.050
his class included
fewer than 2% women.

00:59:38.050 --> 00:59:42.220
When I arrived as MIT's
president, over 45%

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:45.400
of the entering
freshmen were women.

00:59:45.400 --> 00:59:50.860
Paul had primed the Institute
for a woman president.

00:59:50.860 --> 00:59:53.050
In many critically
important ways,

00:59:53.050 --> 00:59:56.890
Paul accelerated MIT's rise
to world class excellence

00:59:56.890 --> 01:00:01.000
in biology and fostered
the remarkable development

01:00:01.000 --> 01:00:04.190
of the Kendall Square
innovation cluster.

01:00:04.190 --> 01:00:07.150
Now, you might well accuse
me of seeing the world

01:00:07.150 --> 01:00:11.410
through biology-tinted
glasses, but 2020 hindsight

01:00:11.410 --> 01:00:13.900
shows clearly that
Paul recognized early

01:00:13.900 --> 01:00:16.180
on the growing
importance and long range

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:20.500
possibilities of the
molecular biology revolution.

01:00:20.500 --> 01:00:23.410
The biology department had
adopted this revolutionary way

01:00:23.410 --> 01:00:27.520
of studying the living world
with a kind of monomania,

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:30.520
propelling the Institute to
national and international

01:00:30.520 --> 01:00:31.990
leadership

01:00:31.990 --> 01:00:36.220
Paul actively fostered the
department's many offshoots.

01:00:36.220 --> 01:00:38.410
The Center for Cancer
Research was launched

01:00:38.410 --> 01:00:40.170
when Paul was chancellor.

01:00:40.170 --> 01:00:44.770
And as president, he navigated
the complex and sometimes vexed

01:00:44.770 --> 01:00:48.160
discussions that led to the
establishment of the Whitehead

01:00:48.160 --> 01:00:51.370
Institute for
Biomedical Research.

01:00:51.370 --> 01:00:55.490
In the words of Institute
Professor Phillip Sharpe,

01:00:55.490 --> 01:00:59.770
Paul made the Whitehead happen.

01:00:59.770 --> 01:01:02.440
Paul provided a new building,
of which there were not many

01:01:02.440 --> 01:01:05.650
during his presidency, for
the biology department.

01:01:05.650 --> 01:01:09.250
And perhaps more significantly,
for MIT's definition

01:01:09.250 --> 01:01:10.930
of ourselves.

01:01:10.930 --> 01:01:14.500
He supported the adoption
of the biology requirement

01:01:14.500 --> 01:01:17.500
for all undergraduates,
setting biology

01:01:17.500 --> 01:01:21.550
on equal footing among
MIT's canonical triumvirate

01:01:21.550 --> 01:01:25.060
of physics, man, and chemistry.

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:28.060
Taken together, Paul
set conditions in place

01:01:28.060 --> 01:01:32.950
for the biological, biomedical,
biotech explosion at MIT

01:01:32.950 --> 01:01:35.040
and in Kendall Square.

01:01:35.040 --> 01:01:39.230
And in this way, too,
he primed the Institute

01:01:39.230 --> 01:01:43.640
for its first
biologist president.

01:01:43.640 --> 01:01:48.800
Always the teacher, Paul
orchestrated a magnificent gift

01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:52.590
to me in my first
months on campus.

01:01:52.590 --> 01:01:56.030
He gathered his class
of 54 colleagues

01:01:56.030 --> 01:02:01.310
and their newly donned
cardinal jackets to meet me.

01:02:01.310 --> 01:02:04.280
Our dinner together taught
me more about the tradition,

01:02:04.280 --> 01:02:07.490
devotion, and pride
carried by MIT alumni,

01:02:07.490 --> 01:02:10.970
than any abstract
description could have done.

01:02:10.970 --> 01:02:15.650
As the adage about teaching
goes, he didn't tell me,

01:02:15.650 --> 01:02:17.960
he showed me.

01:02:17.960 --> 01:02:20.840
Many who attended that
dinner are here with us

01:02:20.840 --> 01:02:22.320
this afternoon.

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:24.850
And I would say, again,
what I said that evening.

01:02:28.230 --> 01:02:31.560
Thank you, Paul, for
bringing us together

01:02:31.560 --> 01:02:37.190
in support of MIT's core
values, the pursuit of truth,

01:02:37.190 --> 01:02:42.830
meritocracy, personal integrity,
and service to others.

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:50.320
And let me say again, for
showing us rather than telling

01:02:50.320 --> 01:02:54.539
us these core values,
we thank you, Paul.

01:03:21.020 --> 01:03:23.820
I am Gerald Wilson.

01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:27.960
And I served as
dean of engineering

01:03:27.960 --> 01:03:29.424
while Paul was president.

01:03:34.530 --> 01:03:39.690
Each of us have memories of
our interaction with Paul Gray

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:44.940
and how he impacted our lives
and our experience at MIT.

01:03:44.940 --> 01:03:47.490
I first saw Paul when
he was a teaching

01:03:47.490 --> 01:03:51.780
assistant in a junior
year subject I was taking.

01:03:51.780 --> 01:03:54.630
He was demonstrating
electromechanical principles

01:03:54.630 --> 01:03:59.910
on a rotating machine in a
building three lecture hall.

01:03:59.910 --> 01:04:02.250
He was an early influence
in the Department

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:04.140
of Electrical Engineering.

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:07.590
When electronics was
transitioning from vacuum tube

01:04:07.590 --> 01:04:10.560
devices to
semiconductor devices,

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:13.680
he coauthored four
of the seven texts

01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:16.710
which were some of the
earliest teaching tools that

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:19.260
enabled that transition.

01:04:19.260 --> 01:04:22.560
Later, after he
was president, he

01:04:22.560 --> 01:04:26.130
lectured a sophomore
electronics subject.

01:04:26.130 --> 01:04:30.930
I remember his demonstrating
negative feedback in a circuit

01:04:30.930 --> 01:04:33.540
and his pointing out
that negative feedback,

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:36.930
if used carefully, could
be a positive factor

01:04:36.930 --> 01:04:41.820
in social situations as well.

01:04:41.820 --> 01:04:46.380
His style, whether teaching
in a formal classroom lecture

01:04:46.380 --> 01:04:50.280
or explaining his views on
some administrative manner,

01:04:50.280 --> 01:04:54.960
was characterized, as you heard,
by careful, methodical, and

01:04:54.960 --> 01:04:57.480
thoughtful descriptions.

01:04:57.480 --> 01:05:02.640
He was always approachable
and receptive of questions.

01:05:02.640 --> 01:05:05.760
Even in a lecture hall
with 200 students,

01:05:05.760 --> 01:05:07.590
the environment
he engendered was

01:05:07.590 --> 01:05:11.010
one of sharing what he
knew with many students

01:05:11.010 --> 01:05:14.850
engaging him after a
lecture to ask questions.

01:05:14.850 --> 01:05:20.750
He was a warm, dedicated,
and effective teacher.

01:05:20.750 --> 01:05:25.940
Early on, Paul was looked to
for his wisdom and advice.

01:05:25.940 --> 01:05:30.590
His careful, thoughtful, and
earnest approach to a question

01:05:30.590 --> 01:05:35.260
was very often reflected
in his facial expression.

01:05:35.260 --> 01:05:38.650
You could see his effort to
provide a thoughtful response

01:05:38.650 --> 01:05:43.570
to your question, even
in casual conversation.

01:05:43.570 --> 01:05:47.140
During the Vietnam War era,
there was much protesting

01:05:47.140 --> 01:05:52.300
and anger among our
students and our faculty.

01:05:52.300 --> 01:05:56.700
We had an AWOL soldier
ensconced in the student center.

01:05:56.700 --> 01:06:00.070
300 state and city
police marched down

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:03.280
Massachusetts Avenue
in [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:03.280 --> 01:06:06.280
to confront students
blocking employees

01:06:06.280 --> 01:06:08.950
in the instrumentation
laboratory.

01:06:08.950 --> 01:06:14.600
Students occupied the
president's office throughout.

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:18.650
Paul was often a calming
influence during this period.

01:06:18.650 --> 01:06:22.070
Seeking the best result
for those involved,

01:06:22.070 --> 01:06:26.060
he focused on allowing
protest without allowing

01:06:26.060 --> 01:06:29.400
it to destroy the institution.

01:06:29.400 --> 01:06:31.770
He was always approachable.

01:06:31.770 --> 01:06:33.750
He seemed to
remember everyone he

01:06:33.750 --> 01:06:37.500
had met by name, whether
a student, a researcher,

01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:41.220
a support staff, or
a faculty member.

01:06:41.220 --> 01:06:46.290
He encouraged people to call
him Paul, putting aside titles.

01:06:46.290 --> 01:06:49.320
And that is how he
was addressed by many,

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:55.300
a measure of how he was viewed
and trusted by the community.

01:06:55.300 --> 01:06:57.990
And with that, he
engendered and nourished

01:06:57.990 --> 01:07:02.220
a sense of community
that most everyone felt,

01:07:02.220 --> 01:07:07.110
a sense of belonging to a place
that strived for excellence,

01:07:07.110 --> 01:07:10.140
a place you could trust.

01:07:10.140 --> 01:07:13.680
He was clearly a leader,
and behaved as one

01:07:13.680 --> 01:07:19.650
for whom the institution was
deserving of only his best.

01:07:19.650 --> 01:07:21.960
He would carefully
and promptly consider

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:24.720
a proposal brought before him.

01:07:24.720 --> 01:07:26.790
Once he had made a
decision to embrace

01:07:26.790 --> 01:07:32.190
an idea brought by others,
he became their champion.

01:07:32.190 --> 01:07:35.490
Whether it was UROP to embrace
our undergraduates involvement

01:07:35.490 --> 01:07:38.700
in research, a new
building for a department

01:07:38.700 --> 01:07:42.870
or school, a micro-electronics
center, a manufacturing

01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:45.570
initiative, a project
to bring computers

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:49.710
into the hands of students
and faculty, establishing

01:07:49.710 --> 01:07:52.470
an Institute for
biological research

01:07:52.470 --> 01:07:55.590
in the face of considerable
concern and controversy

01:07:55.590 --> 01:07:59.640
on the part of faculty, once
he was convinced that it had

01:07:59.640 --> 01:08:03.180
to be done, he put
his own effort into it

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:07.380
by helping to develop a
strategy for a meeting

01:08:07.380 --> 01:08:09.550
with prospective donors.

01:08:09.550 --> 01:08:12.910
He was so much more than
the approving executive.

01:08:12.910 --> 01:08:18.149
He was a hands-on supporter
and builder of the initiative.

01:08:18.149 --> 01:08:22.800
One time I had won his
support for an initiative

01:08:22.800 --> 01:08:27.359
that was going to require
$18 million in funding.

01:08:27.359 --> 01:08:32.370
Months later, I had to
return to inform him

01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:35.490
that the cost was to double.

01:08:35.490 --> 01:08:40.819
I had expected a disappointed
reaction, at least,

01:08:40.819 --> 01:08:44.250
and a question of whether
we should proceed.

01:08:44.250 --> 01:08:47.729
Instead, he characterized
it as unfortunate,

01:08:47.729 --> 01:08:51.600
but then went on to help me
identify ways that could still

01:08:51.600 --> 01:08:56.370
make it happen, no
recrimination, just support

01:08:56.370 --> 01:08:59.939
and help and creatively
solving the problem.

01:08:59.939 --> 01:09:03.750
I always felt that
he had in my back.

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:07.080
Paul had a consistent and deep
concern for the experience

01:09:07.080 --> 01:09:10.890
that MIT provided for our
undergraduate students.

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:13.710
With Priscilla's
strong involvement,

01:09:13.710 --> 01:09:16.189
he addressed significant
issues that affected

01:09:16.189 --> 01:09:18.870
the undergraduate environment.

01:09:18.870 --> 01:09:21.420
Early on, as you've
heard, he became concerned

01:09:21.420 --> 01:09:28.200
that there were so few women in
MIT's undergraduate population.

01:09:28.200 --> 01:09:31.950
He learned that
the cause, in part,

01:09:31.950 --> 01:09:35.430
was a lack of adequate
housing for women.

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:37.290
Women's admissions
were restricted

01:09:37.290 --> 01:09:40.080
due to the limited space
of a women's dormitory

01:09:40.080 --> 01:09:42.210
over in Boston.

01:09:42.210 --> 01:09:45.330
He took action and
removed that restriction.

01:09:45.330 --> 01:09:47.729
And with a gift from
Helen McCormack,

01:09:47.729 --> 01:09:53.010
provided significantly larger
dormitory space for women.

01:09:53.010 --> 01:09:57.150
Paul also addressed MIT's lack
of a strong minority contingent

01:09:57.150 --> 01:09:59.520
in the undergraduate class.

01:09:59.520 --> 01:10:02.700
Before he was the president, he
aggressively worked with others

01:10:02.700 --> 01:10:06.480
to change our approach by
seeking out and attracting

01:10:06.480 --> 01:10:10.560
minorities, not just
waiting for them to apply.

01:10:10.560 --> 01:10:14.610
Changing our environment in this
regard had its difficulties.

01:10:14.610 --> 01:10:16.950
We had to learn how
to identify students

01:10:16.950 --> 01:10:18.900
who could succeed here.

01:10:18.900 --> 01:10:22.770
But Paul persevered and
stuck to his core beliefs

01:10:22.770 --> 01:10:26.460
that we could make MIT a
place where a diverse student

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:29.010
body could succeed.

01:10:29.010 --> 01:10:32.070
By the time he completed
his presidency,

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:34.890
there was a sea change
in the environment,

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:39.660
with significantly more minority
and women undergraduates.

01:10:39.660 --> 01:10:43.890
He drove MIT to be a
welcoming and successful place

01:10:43.890 --> 01:10:48.240
for a diverse
undergraduate student body.

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:52.860
With Priscilla, they made MIT
a more welcoming environment

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:54.960
for our undergraduates.

01:10:54.960 --> 01:10:58.500
They held dinners at Gray House
for the entire senior class

01:10:58.500 --> 01:11:03.300
each year, hosting 70 or
more students at a time.

01:11:03.300 --> 01:11:07.230
He and Priscilla engendered
a welcoming spirit and tone

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:10.140
that permeated the
place in ways that

01:11:10.140 --> 01:11:16.140
continue to have an everlasting
effect on the entire community.

01:11:16.140 --> 01:11:19.370
How does one summarize
the life of the man who,

01:11:19.370 --> 01:11:24.420
with so much dedication, did
so much for this institution?

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:27.720
In that effort, I was
reminded of the spirit

01:11:27.720 --> 01:11:33.200
of a verse that is often used
in a Jewish memorial service.

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:38.910
For the creativity, quality,
and care in his teaching,

01:11:38.910 --> 01:11:40.950
we remember him.

01:11:40.950 --> 01:11:44.610
For his leadership, guidance,
and calming influence in times

01:11:44.610 --> 01:11:47.910
of strife, we remember him.

01:11:47.910 --> 01:11:50.750
For strengthening our
sense of community,

01:11:50.750 --> 01:11:57.270
for making MIT a place of trust
and honesty, we remember him.

01:11:57.270 --> 01:12:01.290
For his encouragement, support,
and championship for the work

01:12:01.290 --> 01:12:04.080
of others, we remember him.

01:12:04.080 --> 01:12:07.830
For his friendship,
we remember him.

01:12:07.830 --> 01:12:11.400
For his commitment,
concern for, and dedication

01:12:11.400 --> 01:12:16.140
to the environment for our
students, we remember him.

01:12:16.140 --> 01:12:18.600
For his leadership,
drive, and commitment

01:12:18.600 --> 01:12:21.920
for attracting more women
and minority students,

01:12:21.920 --> 01:12:25.680
and to assuring that they
can seed succeed here,

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:27.840
we remember him.

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:30.810
For all of his
efforts and commitment

01:12:30.810 --> 01:12:35.340
to MIT, to which he
so fondly referred to

01:12:35.340 --> 01:12:39.030
as this special place,
we remember him.

01:13:25.800 --> 01:13:30.120
Today, we gather to honor
and celebrate my dad.

01:13:30.120 --> 01:13:32.895
And I thank all of
you for being here.

01:13:35.490 --> 01:13:42.180
This event means so much to
my mother and the Gray family.

01:13:42.180 --> 01:13:46.600
On a point of
perspective, I would

01:13:46.600 --> 01:13:51.460
like to point out,
however, that this is more

01:13:51.460 --> 01:13:54.865
than a celebration
of a life well lived.

01:13:57.860 --> 01:14:00.860
If we are to honor
my father and what

01:14:00.860 --> 01:14:06.540
he meant to this
institution, we must also

01:14:06.540 --> 01:14:12.630
recognize what this
institution meant to him.

01:14:12.630 --> 01:14:19.630
I think that today we gather
to celebrate MIT as well.

01:14:19.630 --> 01:14:21.510
We've heard it
several times today,

01:14:21.510 --> 01:14:25.880
my dad called MIT
this special place.

01:14:25.880 --> 01:14:29.190
And I think that all of
us here today are blessed

01:14:29.190 --> 01:14:33.030
in some special, precious way.

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:39.240
Whether you teach, learn,
research, administrate, clean,

01:14:39.240 --> 01:14:43.200
maintain, or facilitate
the workings of the school

01:14:43.200 --> 01:14:47.620
in some non-academic
way, or just

01:14:47.620 --> 01:14:52.080
to love someone
who does, or did,

01:14:52.080 --> 01:14:56.310
I think it is hard not to
be touched by the power

01:14:56.310 --> 01:14:57.890
and promise of MIT.

01:15:00.490 --> 01:15:03.970
I grew up in a house
where we did not

01:15:03.970 --> 01:15:08.350
know the position of the Red
Sox in the AL East standings.

01:15:11.030 --> 01:15:13.370
I did not know what
the Stanley Cup

01:15:13.370 --> 01:15:17.970
was until the Globe published
that iconic photograph of Bobby

01:15:17.970 --> 01:15:23.270
Orr parallel to the ice.

01:15:23.270 --> 01:15:27.050
But I did know who had
won the Nobel prizes

01:15:27.050 --> 01:15:29.235
and what schools they worked at.

01:15:29.235 --> 01:15:35.540
[LAUGHING]

01:15:35.540 --> 01:15:43.260
Shortly after dad's passing,
when my cell phone dinged,

01:15:43.260 --> 01:15:45.930
you know, those
noises, alert noises,

01:15:45.930 --> 01:15:50.400
with the news alert that
Doctor Rainer Weiss had

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:54.990
won this year's prize in
physics, along with two

01:15:54.990 --> 01:16:01.920
of his colleagues from another
school that shall not be named,

01:16:01.920 --> 01:16:04.560
I had to smile.

01:16:04.560 --> 01:16:08.790
This would have
pleased dad so much.

01:16:08.790 --> 01:16:18.290
If dad were alive and we were
in an earlier, better day,

01:16:18.290 --> 01:16:19.850
the race would
have been on to see

01:16:19.850 --> 01:16:24.130
if I could get the news
to him before he heard.

01:16:24.130 --> 01:16:26.380
Of course, I would
have lost that race.

01:16:26.380 --> 01:16:29.740
MIT always had better
communications technology.

01:16:32.870 --> 01:16:35.140
I believe that a
big part of what

01:16:35.140 --> 01:16:41.630
made MIT so special
for my dad was that it

01:16:41.630 --> 01:16:46.550
was the land of his heroes.

01:16:46.550 --> 01:16:49.700
For the Gray kids,
or the core four,

01:16:49.700 --> 01:16:55.780
as we like to call ourselves,
this aspect of my dad's life

01:16:55.780 --> 01:16:59.890
was completely
transparent and open.

01:16:59.890 --> 01:17:06.020
These incredible people were
also the titans in our lives.

01:17:06.020 --> 01:17:11.230
I visited Doc
Edgerton in his lab.

01:17:11.230 --> 01:17:13.480
We saw her image
tonight and I think

01:17:13.480 --> 01:17:19.940
I've heard her name mentioned
at least three times tonight.

01:17:19.940 --> 01:17:24.060
But I used to spend weekends
doing chores with Dr. Margaret

01:17:24.060 --> 01:17:27.750
MacVicar on her farm.

01:17:27.750 --> 01:17:32.490
And in high school, I spoke
with Dr. Jerry Wiesner

01:17:32.490 --> 01:17:35.770
when I waited for dad
in the suite of offices

01:17:35.770 --> 01:17:38.800
that they shared,
almost every day.

01:17:43.130 --> 01:17:46.370
And perhaps most importantly,
our big brother, Jim Taylor,

01:17:46.370 --> 01:17:49.700
class of '65, taught us
kids the Texas Two Step.

01:17:53.030 --> 01:17:55.250
When my family was
last all together,

01:17:55.250 --> 01:17:58.040
we gathered at Hancock
Church in remembrance of dad.

01:18:00.580 --> 01:18:04.600
On that day, Dr. Reif
flew in from welcoming

01:18:04.600 --> 01:18:08.170
his third grandchild
to this world

01:18:08.170 --> 01:18:12.610
to make sure that he was
there to sit with my mother.

01:18:12.610 --> 01:18:17.520
Dr. Jackson, another touchstone
in the tapestry of my father's

01:18:17.520 --> 01:18:22.590
life, was also there from great
distance to be with my mother.

01:18:22.590 --> 01:18:24.900
And of course, Jim
Taylor, who had

01:18:24.900 --> 01:18:27.830
flown in upon my dad's
passing to spend three days

01:18:27.830 --> 01:18:30.900
with my mother, had returned.

01:18:30.900 --> 01:18:33.300
It would be impossible
to adequately express

01:18:33.300 --> 01:18:42.670
the gratitude of my
family for all of you,

01:18:42.670 --> 01:18:48.340
and the countless colleagues,
friends, and students.

01:18:48.340 --> 01:18:55.870
We are profoundly
grateful for all

01:18:55.870 --> 01:19:03.210
that you have done for my dad,
both in life and in death.

01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:13.360
MIT truly is this special place.

01:19:13.360 --> 01:19:15.450
It's its people that make it so.

01:19:17.990 --> 01:19:19.820
As I prepared to
speak today, I thought

01:19:19.820 --> 01:19:23.540
about different approaches
to these comments.

01:19:23.540 --> 01:19:29.720
Ultimately the most visceral
lesson of these past few months

01:19:29.720 --> 01:19:31.550
was what a humbling
experience it

01:19:31.550 --> 01:19:36.920
has been, humbling
to see dad go,

01:19:36.920 --> 01:19:39.567
humbling to see him
make it OK for us.

01:19:43.770 --> 01:19:50.370
Humbling to realize that my
thinking that the inevitable

01:19:50.370 --> 01:19:56.490
cannot be escaped and that I
was OK with all of this was mere

01:19:56.490 --> 01:19:59.610
arrogance.

01:19:59.610 --> 01:20:03.810
Humbling to realize that I
don't have any wisdom except

01:20:03.810 --> 01:20:10.200
to recommit to being the best
father, husband, brother, son,

01:20:10.200 --> 01:20:15.210
colleague, and school
guide I can be.

01:20:15.210 --> 01:20:18.690
With your forbearance,
I would like

01:20:18.690 --> 01:20:21.930
to share what I
presented at Hancock

01:20:21.930 --> 01:20:25.170
Church a couple of months ago.

01:20:25.170 --> 01:20:28.830
It best contains what I want
to tell you about my dad.

01:20:28.830 --> 01:20:32.640
And I think my mom liked it.

01:20:32.640 --> 01:20:34.140
So we're going to do it again.

01:20:41.030 --> 01:20:46.010
I once asked my dad what the
single most important decision

01:20:46.010 --> 01:20:49.720
of his career at MIT was.

01:20:49.720 --> 01:20:56.570
And as Dr. Wilson
just shared with us,

01:20:56.570 --> 01:21:00.680
when asked that question,
he said, the decision

01:21:00.680 --> 01:21:03.740
to move the women onto
campus here at MIT

01:21:03.740 --> 01:21:09.230
and get rid of that dorm was the
turning point for this school.

01:21:09.230 --> 01:21:14.750
And that closing that
dorm set MIT on its path

01:21:14.750 --> 01:21:18.110
to what it was to become.

01:21:18.110 --> 01:21:23.880
Such a simple thing,
such a powerful thing.

01:21:23.880 --> 01:21:26.610
As a kid, I learned to love
curry at the [INAUDIBLE]

01:21:26.610 --> 01:21:29.310
and tempura at the [INAUDIBLE].

01:21:29.310 --> 01:21:34.020
At MIT events, dinners
hosted at home by my mother,

01:21:34.020 --> 01:21:37.320
and the all important wonderful
and memorable Thanksgivings

01:21:37.320 --> 01:21:40.770
with dad's advisees,
we met people

01:21:40.770 --> 01:21:44.400
from all across America
and all around the world.

01:21:44.400 --> 01:21:47.280
Mom and dad took us to
live in northern Wales

01:21:47.280 --> 01:21:50.280
and we toured Europe
when dad had a break.

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:53.100
My parents came
back from MIT trips

01:21:53.100 --> 01:21:58.890
to places like India,
Egypt, Mexico, China, Japan,

01:21:58.890 --> 01:22:02.600
and shared the wonder of what
they had seen and learned.

01:22:02.600 --> 01:22:05.060
The point I would
like to make is

01:22:05.060 --> 01:22:07.130
that my parents
worked diligently

01:22:07.130 --> 01:22:09.800
and intentionally to
expose us to a world that

01:22:09.800 --> 01:22:13.590
was as big as a MIT.

01:22:13.590 --> 01:22:16.260
It was clear to us that
they valued diversity

01:22:16.260 --> 01:22:19.890
in all its forms, and that
engaging with and learning

01:22:19.890 --> 01:22:22.320
from the broadest
possible group of people

01:22:22.320 --> 01:22:24.450
was something to be
treasured and valued

01:22:24.450 --> 01:22:27.150
as a source of
profound strength.

01:22:30.960 --> 01:22:33.240
As I matured, whether it
was because I was old enough

01:22:33.240 --> 01:22:35.970
to hear certain stories or
whether because I started

01:22:35.970 --> 01:22:39.240
paying attention and linking
things up that we did not

01:22:39.240 --> 01:22:44.220
really talk about as a family,
I came to understand that, based

01:22:44.220 --> 01:22:46.560
on the context and
the value system

01:22:46.560 --> 01:22:50.070
that my dad was
raised in, he would

01:22:50.070 --> 01:22:57.220
have seemed a very unlikely
champion of diversity.

01:22:57.220 --> 01:22:59.580
After my kids were
born, I decided

01:22:59.580 --> 01:23:04.350
I needed to ask
my dad about this.

01:23:04.350 --> 01:23:08.200
How had his values
in this regard

01:23:08.200 --> 01:23:13.540
varied so widely from
what he knew as a child.

01:23:13.540 --> 01:23:16.930
While splitting wood down
a Little Compton with him,

01:23:16.930 --> 01:23:21.190
I tried to nicely frame
my central question, which

01:23:21.190 --> 01:23:26.470
boiled down to, hey, dad,
how come you didn't turn out

01:23:26.470 --> 01:23:27.760
to be a bigot?

01:23:27.760 --> 01:23:31.360
[LAUGHING]

01:23:31.360 --> 01:23:32.230
So I asked.

01:23:35.830 --> 01:23:45.520
And he put down the maul, took
off his gloves, wiped his brow,

01:23:45.520 --> 01:23:48.580
and he thought.

01:23:48.580 --> 01:23:53.110
He told me, well,
I guess it just

01:23:53.110 --> 01:23:56.630
never made any sense to me.

01:23:56.630 --> 01:24:00.570
He put his gloves back on
and we went back to work.

01:24:04.390 --> 01:24:06.850
Such a simple thing.

01:24:06.850 --> 01:24:08.080
Such a powerful thing.

01:24:10.900 --> 01:24:13.150
As a dyslexic kid
that did not learn

01:24:13.150 --> 01:24:15.490
to read until the good
folks at the Carroll School

01:24:15.490 --> 01:24:19.600
taught me to mainstream
standardized academic success

01:24:19.600 --> 01:24:21.410
was a stranger to me.

01:24:21.410 --> 01:24:24.220
I failed kindergarten.

01:24:24.220 --> 01:24:27.970
Not an easy thing to do.

01:24:27.970 --> 01:24:32.230
I got bounced out of chemistry
as a junior in high school

01:24:32.230 --> 01:24:36.550
because the whole mole
thing completely eluded me.

01:24:36.550 --> 01:24:38.890
And I only graduated
from high school

01:24:38.890 --> 01:24:42.280
because Rosemary Sauerman of
Hancock Church intensively

01:24:42.280 --> 01:24:45.400
tutored me in German
and helped me eke out

01:24:45.400 --> 01:24:50.770
a 60.25% average for the year.

01:24:50.770 --> 01:24:53.380
It was not really until
college, and more particularly

01:24:53.380 --> 01:24:57.170
graduate school, where I was
able to play to my strengths

01:24:57.170 --> 01:24:59.530
that I experienced
solid academic success

01:24:59.530 --> 01:25:02.080
and truly enjoyed school.

01:25:02.080 --> 01:25:05.650
Now juxtapose that with my dad.

01:25:05.650 --> 01:25:11.080
Clearly one of the best and most
talented minds that many of us

01:25:11.080 --> 01:25:15.160
will know in our lifetimes.

01:25:15.160 --> 01:25:20.290
He could have crushed me with
a simple ill-chosen word.

01:25:22.840 --> 01:25:23.560
He never did.

01:25:26.190 --> 01:25:30.810
To be clear, there were no
free passes in the Gray house.

01:25:30.810 --> 01:25:33.330
All report cards were
reviewed with dad

01:25:33.330 --> 01:25:36.360
and discussed in detail.

01:25:36.360 --> 01:25:38.550
But dad always
threaded that needle

01:25:38.550 --> 01:25:40.470
between exhorting
me to do my best

01:25:40.470 --> 01:25:44.880
and holding me accountable
while building my confidence

01:25:44.880 --> 01:25:49.630
and honoring my success
in the face of adversity.

01:25:49.630 --> 01:25:53.600
I am profoundly
grateful for that.

01:25:53.600 --> 01:25:55.960
I can remember MIT
events and gatherings

01:25:55.960 --> 01:25:59.920
where well-intentioned
people would ask me

01:25:59.920 --> 01:26:04.870
what I was going to
study when I went to MIT.

01:26:04.870 --> 01:26:09.580
MIT was clearly not on
my life choice menu,

01:26:09.580 --> 01:26:13.600
nor did it align with my
strengths and interests.

01:26:13.600 --> 01:26:17.710
I remember one particularly
robust grilling

01:26:17.710 --> 01:26:22.570
by an alumni of my father's
generation, who clearly thought

01:26:22.570 --> 01:26:24.370
that the son of
Paul Gray must be

01:26:24.370 --> 01:26:31.210
a regular chip off the old block
and ready to take MIT by storm.

01:26:31.210 --> 01:26:35.840
As this gentleman talked,
out of the corner of my eye,

01:26:35.840 --> 01:26:37.030
I saw my dad.

01:26:37.030 --> 01:26:43.501
And as I focused on him, he
gave me his, I am proud of you,

01:26:43.501 --> 01:26:44.000
smile.

01:26:46.980 --> 01:26:49.430
Such a simple thing.

01:26:49.430 --> 01:26:53.040
Such a powerful thing.

01:26:53.040 --> 01:26:56.460
Dad was often asked for advice
on how to be successful.

01:26:56.460 --> 01:27:00.570
His response was consistent.

01:27:00.570 --> 01:27:04.590
He would suggest that you needed
to find what interested you

01:27:04.590 --> 01:27:07.920
most and commit
yourself to it fully,

01:27:07.920 --> 01:27:10.830
and the rest would
take care of itself.

01:27:10.830 --> 01:27:17.620
At a very real level, this was
extremely uncomfortable advice.

01:27:17.620 --> 01:27:21.580
Wouldn't it be easier if there
were just a school to attend,

01:27:21.580 --> 01:27:26.500
a few courses to take,
or books to read.

01:27:26.500 --> 01:27:30.640
Dad's advice was premised
on self-awareness,

01:27:30.640 --> 01:27:33.700
thinking for
yourself, as well as

01:27:33.700 --> 01:27:37.510
being accountable to yourself.

01:27:37.510 --> 01:27:40.350
My dad spent, with the
exception of his service

01:27:40.350 --> 01:27:45.120
with the army, of which
he was extremely proud,

01:27:45.120 --> 01:27:48.200
his entire career at MIT.

01:27:48.200 --> 01:27:50.230
I don't know if you all
know this about him,

01:27:50.230 --> 01:27:53.456
but he never wrote a resume.

01:27:53.456 --> 01:27:56.384
[LAUGHING]

01:27:58.830 --> 01:28:01.830
I once asked him to look
at a resume I had written

01:28:01.830 --> 01:28:05.080
and he said, I really
don't know much about that.

01:28:05.080 --> 01:28:06.420
You'll have to someone else.

01:28:11.470 --> 01:28:15.440
He served MIT with
devotion and passion,

01:28:15.440 --> 01:28:19.760
and was blessed
with the opportunity

01:28:19.760 --> 01:28:22.460
to leave his mark
on the Institution

01:28:22.460 --> 01:28:25.970
at every step of his career.

01:28:25.970 --> 01:28:29.930
Clearly, each day
of his life, My dad

01:28:29.930 --> 01:28:34.920
relentlessly lived the
advice he so often gave.

01:28:34.920 --> 01:28:36.810
Such a simple thing.

01:28:36.810 --> 01:28:40.180
Such a powerful thing.

01:28:40.180 --> 01:28:44.340
Oddly, in thinking
about all the times

01:28:44.340 --> 01:28:49.040
I heard him asked
about success, I never

01:28:49.040 --> 01:28:53.900
heard anyone ask him for
advice on how to be happy.

01:28:53.900 --> 01:28:56.870
Of course, I believe he
would have given essentially

01:28:56.870 --> 01:29:00.320
the same answer, but
just extended the scope

01:29:00.320 --> 01:29:03.770
to include a spouse
and all the family,

01:29:03.770 --> 01:29:08.120
love, and power that arises
from and around the union of two

01:29:08.120 --> 01:29:10.130
people.

01:29:10.130 --> 01:29:14.180
I believe that, if asked, all
the core four would tell you

01:29:14.180 --> 01:29:17.750
that my father's devotion
to my mother, and of course

01:29:17.750 --> 01:29:21.140
her's to him, has
provided the foundation

01:29:21.140 --> 01:29:25.610
in the framework for the
lives that we and our kids

01:29:25.610 --> 01:29:28.570
live today.

01:29:28.570 --> 01:29:35.580
A father's love, such a simple
thing, such a powerful thing.

01:29:35.580 --> 01:29:37.487
Thank you.

01:29:37.487 --> 01:29:40.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:35:00.430 --> 01:35:03.830
My name is Larry
Bacow, class of '72.

01:35:03.830 --> 01:35:06.440
And like many of the
people in this room,

01:35:06.440 --> 01:35:09.010
I was a student of Paul's.

01:35:09.010 --> 01:35:12.550
However, it was not
in core six, rather,

01:35:12.550 --> 01:35:17.140
Paul helped to teach me how
to be a university president.

01:35:17.140 --> 01:35:19.780
Shortly after I was
named president of Tufts,

01:35:19.780 --> 01:35:23.970
Paul told me what my
new life would be like.

01:35:23.970 --> 01:35:26.760
Running a research
university, he said,

01:35:26.760 --> 01:35:30.330
was like trying to
drive an 18 wheeler down

01:35:30.330 --> 01:35:35.130
an icy, hilly, mountain
road with multiple hairpin

01:35:35.130 --> 01:35:41.150
turns, no guardrails,
and 1,000 foot drop offs.

01:35:41.150 --> 01:35:44.700
And if that wasn't hard enough,
faculty have their hands

01:35:44.700 --> 01:35:49.490
on the wheel, students have
their foot on the accelerator,

01:35:49.490 --> 01:35:53.100
and alumni have their
foot on the brake.

01:35:53.100 --> 01:35:57.668
And you are responsible
for the outcome.

01:35:57.668 --> 01:36:00.080
[LAUGHING]

01:36:00.080 --> 01:36:04.340
Paul had a pithy way
of making his point.

01:36:04.340 --> 01:36:07.130
I was to receive all
sorts of good advice

01:36:07.130 --> 01:36:11.180
from him as I prepared to leave
the comfortable surroundings

01:36:11.180 --> 01:36:14.270
of MIT for the wilds of Medford.

01:36:16.940 --> 01:36:20.900
Before you meet with
anyone ask yourself

01:36:20.900 --> 01:36:24.170
whose job are you doing now.

01:36:24.170 --> 01:36:27.050
Very good advice for a
university president, right?

01:36:27.050 --> 01:36:32.600
Shirley and Susan-- one
of Paul's favorites.

01:36:32.600 --> 01:36:35.270
He used to say, you know,
you need to pat people

01:36:35.270 --> 01:36:40.280
10 times on the back for every
time you kick them in the rear.

01:36:43.460 --> 01:36:47.940
Nothing succeeds like
a successor, very true.

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:51.700
[LAUGHING]

01:36:51.700 --> 01:36:55.660
If you can't get your work
done in 24 hours in a day,

01:36:55.660 --> 01:36:56.900
start working nights.

01:36:59.940 --> 01:37:03.180
And Adele's favorite,
and Priscilla,

01:37:03.180 --> 01:37:06.390
she's sorry she can't
be here with me today,

01:37:06.390 --> 01:37:10.290
but her absolute favorite,
on any given night,

01:37:10.290 --> 01:37:13.020
you will have four
or five invitations.

01:37:13.020 --> 01:37:15.570
So you're going to be
disappointing three or four

01:37:15.570 --> 01:37:18.380
different people or groups.

01:37:18.380 --> 01:37:22.980
My advice to you, at
least one night a week,

01:37:22.980 --> 01:37:26.880
disappoint all of them.

01:37:26.880 --> 01:37:30.000
I could go on.

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:33.090
David Reismann, the Harvard
sociologist and student

01:37:33.090 --> 01:37:37.320
of higher education, used to
say that university presidents

01:37:37.320 --> 01:37:43.650
become the living, walking
logos of their institutions.

01:37:43.650 --> 01:37:46.430
In reflecting upon
this statement,

01:37:46.430 --> 01:37:50.720
I don't think any president
of any institution

01:37:50.720 --> 01:37:54.710
ever represented the
values of a place

01:37:54.710 --> 01:37:58.010
better than Paul did here.

01:37:58.010 --> 01:38:01.080
Paul Gray was MIT.

01:38:03.590 --> 01:38:08.430
He was incredibly smart
but also incredibly modest.

01:38:08.430 --> 01:38:11.830
He was tough but caring.

01:38:11.830 --> 01:38:16.030
He took his work seriously
but never himself.

01:38:16.030 --> 01:38:19.720
He was comfortable
greeting heads of state

01:38:19.720 --> 01:38:23.350
but fundamentally
he was blue collar.

01:38:23.350 --> 01:38:27.820
He was also refreshingly
unpretentious.

01:38:27.820 --> 01:38:30.340
What other college
president adopted

01:38:30.340 --> 01:38:32.980
as their email
address a character

01:38:32.980 --> 01:38:35.950
from a Walt Kelly comic strip?

01:38:35.950 --> 01:38:36.730
pogo@mit.edu.

01:38:40.000 --> 01:38:44.710
As Paul used to say, Pogo
was his favorite philosopher.

01:38:44.710 --> 01:38:47.810
Paul also had a
fabulous sense of humor.

01:38:47.810 --> 01:38:52.180
He seemed to have just the
right story for every occasion.

01:38:52.180 --> 01:38:56.830
I cannot tell you how many times
I have repeated his story about

01:38:56.830 --> 01:39:00.730
the Russian peasant encountering
an almost frozen bird.

01:39:00.730 --> 01:39:03.700
You all have heard the story,
it ends with the three lessons.

01:39:03.700 --> 01:39:05.410
I'm not going to
repeat them here.

01:39:05.410 --> 01:39:08.890
Ask me at the reception.

01:39:08.890 --> 01:39:12.160
He also had no difficulty
showing emotion.

01:39:12.160 --> 01:39:15.520
When Paul was
angry, you knew it.

01:39:15.520 --> 01:39:19.510
I recall sitting-- but he
also had a softer side,

01:39:19.510 --> 01:39:22.780
and that is really what,
I think, defined Paul.

01:39:22.780 --> 01:39:27.190
I was call sitting right here
in Kresge auditorium when

01:39:27.190 --> 01:39:30.910
Paul gave a very moving
eulogy for our colleague,

01:39:30.910 --> 01:39:32.880
Jim Cullerton.

01:39:32.880 --> 01:39:37.440
Jim, as many of you may recall,
died well before his time.

01:39:37.440 --> 01:39:40.950
Paul, in that eulogy,
described how closely

01:39:40.950 --> 01:39:45.120
he worked with Jim and
Constantine [INAUDIBLE],,

01:39:45.120 --> 01:39:50.320
another one of our colleagues
who we lost well too soon.

01:39:50.320 --> 01:39:54.150
How they worked so hard to try
and hold this place together

01:39:54.150 --> 01:39:59.520
during the time of student
protests in the late 1960s.

01:39:59.520 --> 01:40:02.990
As Paul recalled
their work together,

01:40:02.990 --> 01:40:09.670
he described the three of
them as a band of brothers.

01:40:09.670 --> 01:40:13.610
And when he did
so, he choked up.

01:40:13.610 --> 01:40:17.330
He loved Jim and he
loved Constantine.

01:40:17.330 --> 01:40:19.970
And he was not afraid to say it.

01:40:19.970 --> 01:40:23.010
And he was not
afraid to show it.

01:40:23.010 --> 01:40:26.880
There was nothing
phony about Paul.

01:40:26.880 --> 01:40:30.480
He was one of the most
real people I have ever

01:40:30.480 --> 01:40:33.796
been privileged to know.

01:40:33.796 --> 01:40:35.420
A number of other
speakers have already

01:40:35.420 --> 01:40:39.920
described Paul's
extraordinary efforts

01:40:39.920 --> 01:40:46.420
to make MIT a better place
for women and for minorities.

01:40:46.420 --> 01:40:49.990
What is less well
known on this campus

01:40:49.990 --> 01:40:53.980
is that Paul was also a
voice for gender diversity

01:40:53.980 --> 01:40:59.500
and inclusion at Priscilla's
Alma Mater, Wheaton College.

01:40:59.500 --> 01:41:03.160
At the same time he
was president of MIT,

01:41:03.160 --> 01:41:06.730
Paul chaired the Wheaton
College Board of Trustees.

01:41:06.730 --> 01:41:10.360
To this day, I cannot imagine
how anybody could have done

01:41:10.360 --> 01:41:12.820
both jobs simultaneously.

01:41:12.820 --> 01:41:15.050
But Paul did.

01:41:15.050 --> 01:41:18.570
And as board chair,
Paul helped to bring

01:41:18.570 --> 01:41:24.630
co-education to Wheaton, a
hugely controversial decision

01:41:24.630 --> 01:41:26.130
at the time.

01:41:26.130 --> 01:41:28.650
Hugely controversial.

01:41:28.650 --> 01:41:33.240
Wheaton, however, is the
thriving, vibrant college

01:41:33.240 --> 01:41:37.250
that it is today
in no small measure

01:41:37.250 --> 01:41:41.100
to Paul's steady and strong
leadership of that board,

01:41:41.100 --> 01:41:44.250
for his willingness to
always do the right thing,

01:41:44.250 --> 01:41:50.520
as has been said before, even
when it was very, very hard.

01:41:50.520 --> 01:41:53.390
But my enduring image
of Paul will always

01:41:53.390 --> 01:41:57.050
be of him walking
hand-in-hand with Priscilla

01:41:57.050 --> 01:41:59.670
across this campus.

01:41:59.670 --> 01:42:04.080
Priscilla, his love
of you knew no bounds.

01:42:04.080 --> 01:42:08.520
Thank you for sharing him
with us for so many years.

01:42:08.520 --> 01:42:11.430
And thank you for
all the two of you

01:42:11.430 --> 01:42:15.520
gave to what Paul, as we've
heard many, many times,

01:42:15.520 --> 01:42:19.990
always called,
this special place.

01:42:19.990 --> 01:42:24.940
What makes it so special
is because people

01:42:24.940 --> 01:42:27.940
like you and Paul.

01:42:27.940 --> 01:42:28.720
Thank you.

01:42:28.720 --> 01:42:30.580
And thank you for
the opportunity

01:42:30.580 --> 01:42:34.050
to be part of this very
special ceremony today.

01:43:12.050 --> 01:43:14.130
My name is Virginia.

01:43:14.130 --> 01:43:16.890
And I am deeply grateful
to President Reif

01:43:16.890 --> 01:43:20.700
for making space
in today's ceremony

01:43:20.700 --> 01:43:24.240
and tribute for a
family perspective

01:43:24.240 --> 01:43:27.060
on and a set of stories
about my father's life

01:43:27.060 --> 01:43:32.300
by allowing Andrew
and me to speak.

01:43:32.300 --> 01:43:37.400
Since MIT was central to
dad's life and love and drive,

01:43:37.400 --> 01:43:40.010
it was central to ours, also.

01:43:40.010 --> 01:43:43.910
Especially as we grow
up in and around campus,

01:43:43.910 --> 01:43:47.930
meeting a wide and fascinating
swath of God's people,

01:43:47.930 --> 01:43:50.990
and always being stretched
to learn and to grow

01:43:50.990 --> 01:43:53.690
and to include the myriad
people that our parents thought

01:43:53.690 --> 01:43:56.450
of as family, too.

01:43:56.450 --> 01:44:00.560
My mother, and all our parent's
children, in-law children,

01:44:00.560 --> 01:44:04.620
grandchildren, and their
spouses, and great grandchild,

01:44:04.620 --> 01:44:07.780
are my father's first family.

01:44:07.780 --> 01:44:11.630
MIT and the community
here is his second.

01:44:11.630 --> 01:44:14.930
My father lived
his life full tilt,

01:44:14.930 --> 01:44:19.130
in incredibly inclusive,
intentional, compassionate,

01:44:19.130 --> 01:44:21.380
and whole-hearted ways.

01:44:21.380 --> 01:44:26.690
Dad lived life in ways that left
no doubt about who he loved,

01:44:26.690 --> 01:44:30.050
what he cared for,
who he stood with,

01:44:30.050 --> 01:44:34.010
what he stood for, who and what
he was willing to work hard

01:44:34.010 --> 01:44:37.850
for, how he felt
about all of us,

01:44:37.850 --> 01:44:44.350
and what he hoped his life
would say after he was gone.

01:44:44.350 --> 01:44:47.080
He was passionate about
his family at home

01:44:47.080 --> 01:44:49.540
and his family in
this special place.

01:44:49.540 --> 01:44:53.830
And he loved the
connections between the two.

01:44:53.830 --> 01:44:57.190
My father squeezed so very
much out of all of his days

01:44:57.190 --> 01:44:58.900
here on earth.

01:44:58.900 --> 01:45:02.920
He was fully present to us and
his life had purpose, passion,

01:45:02.920 --> 01:45:04.450
and focus.

01:45:04.450 --> 01:45:07.630
Dad was all in.

01:45:07.630 --> 01:45:13.250
He used his time, talent,
and treasure for good.

01:45:13.250 --> 01:45:16.340
And even though we can't
really quite yet believe

01:45:16.340 --> 01:45:20.240
that he is gone, I'm
going to share, first,

01:45:20.240 --> 01:45:24.690
on behalf of my amazing
and remarkable sisters.

01:45:24.690 --> 01:45:28.980
Amy first, and
these are her words,

01:45:28.980 --> 01:45:31.560
dad took us, especially
me and Virginia,

01:45:31.560 --> 01:45:35.820
all over the White Mountains and
Snowdonia Such happy memories

01:45:35.820 --> 01:45:38.040
on the trails,
beautiful scenery,

01:45:38.040 --> 01:45:40.350
and views from the top.

01:45:40.350 --> 01:45:44.250
There was a kind of magic
about getting up really early,

01:45:44.250 --> 01:45:47.700
driving to near the mountain,
stopping for breakfast,

01:45:47.700 --> 01:45:49.680
and then doing the climb.

01:45:49.680 --> 01:45:52.470
And since food is always
important to us Grays,

01:45:52.470 --> 01:45:56.160
the lunches and
snacks were awesome.

01:45:56.160 --> 01:45:59.850
Things we didn't usually have,
I remember chocolate bars

01:45:59.850 --> 01:46:02.640
and juicy orange
sections in particular.

01:46:02.640 --> 01:46:04.920
The days were the start
of my deep love affair

01:46:04.920 --> 01:46:06.660
with the great outdoors.

01:46:06.660 --> 01:46:09.840
My own family made camping and
hiking a foundation of our life

01:46:09.840 --> 01:46:11.760
together.

01:46:11.760 --> 01:46:13.590
I keep thinking about
all the woodworking

01:46:13.590 --> 01:46:16.770
that Daddy has done with
all of us over the years.

01:46:16.770 --> 01:46:18.990
I remember the day
when I was little,

01:46:18.990 --> 01:46:20.430
probably eight or
nine years old,

01:46:20.430 --> 01:46:23.880
when he came home from MIT with
a glass-fronted bookcase that

01:46:23.880 --> 01:46:27.000
was being thrown out
from a library at MIT.

01:46:27.000 --> 01:46:29.370
We turned it into my doll house.

01:46:29.370 --> 01:46:32.710
We put wall partitions in
and wallpaper and painted.

01:46:32.710 --> 01:46:35.880
Of course, we even put
electricity in as well.

01:46:39.010 --> 01:46:41.920
It ran on a very large battery.

01:46:41.920 --> 01:46:44.860
I made beaded chandeliers and
we used tiny Christmas tree

01:46:44.860 --> 01:46:46.150
lights for the bulbs.

01:46:46.150 --> 01:46:48.730
It was so much fun
transforming a simple bookcase

01:46:48.730 --> 01:46:53.140
into something I wanted so
much with my father's help.

01:46:53.140 --> 01:46:55.480
Dad worked with my husband,
Dave, on several projects

01:46:55.480 --> 01:46:57.340
at our house in Kennebunk.

01:46:57.340 --> 01:46:59.230
Together, they rebuilt
the screened in porch

01:46:59.230 --> 01:47:01.360
from the ground up to the roof.

01:47:01.360 --> 01:47:04.380
They repaired a very rotten
corner of our garage.

01:47:04.380 --> 01:47:06.310
And over a week, they
redid our kitchen,

01:47:06.310 --> 01:47:09.970
including relocating the
stove, rewiring the kitchen,

01:47:09.970 --> 01:47:12.640
building some new cupboards,
and laying new Formica

01:47:12.640 --> 01:47:14.350
on the countertop.

01:47:14.350 --> 01:47:17.290
Dave loved working with
daddy on these projects.

01:47:17.290 --> 01:47:21.210
No one had ever done that
sort of work with him before.

01:47:21.210 --> 01:47:23.740
Papa and Stephanie
built a bureau together.

01:47:23.740 --> 01:47:26.170
They started with discussing
plans and what she wanted.

01:47:26.170 --> 01:47:28.960
And then they built the whole
thing over a year's time.

01:47:28.960 --> 01:47:30.550
Stephanie loved it.

01:47:30.550 --> 01:47:32.680
And I can attest to the
fact that the drawers still

01:47:32.680 --> 01:47:34.450
pull smoothly.

01:47:34.450 --> 01:47:36.634
It was very well
built. I also know

01:47:36.634 --> 01:47:38.300
that dad worked with
Hanna and Priscilla

01:47:38.300 --> 01:47:40.210
on woodworking projects.

01:47:40.210 --> 01:47:44.350
He loved sharing his skills
and his love of woodworking.

01:47:44.350 --> 01:47:46.460
Working with him over
the past few years,

01:47:46.460 --> 01:47:50.440
it has been very clear to me
how generous he was and is.

01:47:50.440 --> 01:47:52.570
Even as his mind
faded, he was always

01:47:52.570 --> 01:47:54.400
clear about the
organizations that he

01:47:54.400 --> 01:47:57.490
thought were important
to support and why.

01:47:57.490 --> 01:48:00.820
He was also very generous
with his time and energy,

01:48:00.820 --> 01:48:04.450
with the core four going
back to childhood, with help

01:48:04.450 --> 01:48:06.820
on homework or projects.

01:48:06.820 --> 01:48:08.470
And later, with
all of our spouses,

01:48:08.470 --> 01:48:10.840
and our children,
his grandchildren,

01:48:10.840 --> 01:48:14.460
he made himself available
and present in all our lives.

01:48:14.460 --> 01:48:16.630
And this was from a man
had lots of other people

01:48:16.630 --> 01:48:20.500
who wanted his time
and attention also.

01:48:20.500 --> 01:48:22.840
Being together and spending
time knowing each other

01:48:22.840 --> 01:48:25.480
was so important to dad.

01:48:25.480 --> 01:48:28.420
He and mom fostered the love,
friendship, and caring that

01:48:28.420 --> 01:48:31.340
happens between the 26 of us.

01:48:31.340 --> 01:48:35.110
I am deeply thankful for my
entire family and for the bonds

01:48:35.110 --> 01:48:36.730
that we share.

01:48:36.730 --> 01:48:40.450
And I'm deeply grateful to daddy
and mom for giving all of us

01:48:40.450 --> 01:48:43.650
that gift of family.

01:48:43.650 --> 01:48:49.500
Now, in Louise's
words, Weasy, today we

01:48:49.500 --> 01:48:52.470
celebrate my dad,
grandfather to my children,

01:48:52.470 --> 01:48:55.140
a supportive and proud
father-in-law to my husband,

01:48:55.140 --> 01:48:58.170
Tim, and a leader at MIT.

01:48:58.170 --> 01:49:00.360
I want to share memories
I have of growing up

01:49:00.360 --> 01:49:05.340
with dad, Dr. Paul Gray, in our
home life and in our MIT life.

01:49:05.340 --> 01:49:07.630
There was a lot of overlap.

01:49:07.630 --> 01:49:10.170
We had family Friday
date nights, usually

01:49:10.170 --> 01:49:14.070
Regina's pizza in the north end,
or Chinese food, or the faculty

01:49:14.070 --> 01:49:18.300
club in Cambridge, followed
by big ball bowling at MIT

01:49:18.300 --> 01:49:21.660
bowling alleys, or
swimming at the MIT pool,

01:49:21.660 --> 01:49:25.380
the old one, which
now looks very small.

01:49:25.380 --> 01:49:28.590
We loved visiting
Doc Edgerton's lab.

01:49:28.590 --> 01:49:30.660
MIT day camp was a
part of every summer,

01:49:30.660 --> 01:49:34.470
as campers, and also many years
as counselors for some of us.

01:49:34.470 --> 01:49:37.410
All 12 grandchildren visited
mom and dad in the summer

01:49:37.410 --> 01:49:39.300
and loved being campers.

01:49:39.300 --> 01:49:41.550
Over the years, I
walked Gray Way,

01:49:41.550 --> 01:49:44.820
hand-in-hand with my
siblings, mom and dad,

01:49:44.820 --> 01:49:46.530
and the grandchildren.

01:49:46.530 --> 01:49:50.460
The Gray House was our
family home for 10 years.

01:49:50.460 --> 01:49:53.910
It was a welcoming home to
countless family gatherings,

01:49:53.910 --> 01:49:55.840
including three weddings.

01:49:55.840 --> 01:49:58.460
Amy and Dave, Andrew and
[? Yuki, ?] and me and Tim

01:49:58.460 --> 01:50:00.240
all got married there.

01:50:00.240 --> 01:50:03.090
The grandchildren
loved playing in what

01:50:03.090 --> 01:50:04.980
was known as the
Green forest, which

01:50:04.980 --> 01:50:07.560
is a space near the public
bathrooms on the bottom floor

01:50:07.560 --> 01:50:09.900
of the Gray House.

01:50:09.900 --> 01:50:12.660
Now the rug is white.

01:50:12.660 --> 01:50:16.150
It's probably lucky
the kids have gone.

01:50:16.150 --> 01:50:19.170
We had two inaugurations to
attend, chancellor and then

01:50:19.170 --> 01:50:22.080
President, and both were held
in the truly amazing Gray

01:50:22.080 --> 01:50:23.790
courtyard.

01:50:23.790 --> 01:50:26.255
I have very many fond
memories of Gray House.

01:50:26.255 --> 01:50:28.380
I spent a lot of time there,
more than my siblings,

01:50:28.380 --> 01:50:30.630
since I am the baby.

01:50:30.630 --> 01:50:33.720
I went to Leslie University
at the other end of Cambridge.

01:50:33.720 --> 01:50:35.820
I recall coming home
one Friday afternoon

01:50:35.820 --> 01:50:38.864
to go on a date with my dad.

01:50:38.864 --> 01:50:40.530
I was unaware of the
Corporation meeting

01:50:40.530 --> 01:50:42.750
in the large living
room on the first floor.

01:50:42.750 --> 01:50:46.510
I came in singing,
anyone home who loves me?

01:50:49.980 --> 01:50:53.310
At the top of my lungs.

01:50:53.310 --> 01:50:57.030
To my surprise, and also his,
I encountered the treasurer

01:50:57.030 --> 01:51:00.540
of the Corporation at that
time in the front hall.

01:51:00.540 --> 01:51:04.050
He encouraged me to
be a little quieter.

01:51:04.050 --> 01:51:06.840
But dad came around the
corner with a giant dad hug.

01:51:06.840 --> 01:51:09.000
He introduced me
to the Corporation.

01:51:09.000 --> 01:51:12.240
He made it comfortable and
not so embarrassing for me.

01:51:12.240 --> 01:51:13.890
He always loved
having his kids around

01:51:13.890 --> 01:51:16.170
to introduce to his MIT family.

01:51:16.170 --> 01:51:19.750
His love and pride
were always tangible.

01:51:19.750 --> 01:51:21.760
I'm sure all my
siblings, our spouses,

01:51:21.760 --> 01:51:24.460
and the grandchildren
will agree that education

01:51:24.460 --> 01:51:26.260
was highly encouraged.

01:51:26.260 --> 01:51:28.660
Find your passion, what
interests you, what you

01:51:28.660 --> 01:51:30.830
are good at, and go after it.

01:51:30.830 --> 01:51:34.090
Don't be afraid to step out
of your comfort zone, either.

01:51:34.090 --> 01:51:35.890
He encouraged the
core four to pursue

01:51:35.890 --> 01:51:37.930
our degrees and our interests.

01:51:37.930 --> 01:51:40.180
And mom and dad always
encouraged us and our partners

01:51:40.180 --> 01:51:42.160
to go after their interests.

01:51:42.160 --> 01:51:45.280
Our endeavors included higher
degrees, professional goals,

01:51:45.280 --> 01:51:46.540
and hobbies.

01:51:46.540 --> 01:51:49.240
Dad also enjoyed participating
in many of our interests

01:51:49.240 --> 01:51:53.680
with us, including fishing
with Tim and Andrew, home

01:51:53.680 --> 01:51:56.980
improvements, travel,
food, wine, hiking

01:51:56.980 --> 01:51:59.470
and biking, historical
field trips,

01:51:59.470 --> 01:52:01.810
and books on diverse topics.

01:52:01.810 --> 01:52:03.790
However, I do not
believe he ever

01:52:03.790 --> 01:52:06.850
knitted with us Gray ladies,
even though we invited

01:52:06.850 --> 01:52:10.120
him to join us many times.

01:52:10.120 --> 01:52:13.900
I want all of you to know
that my dad and mom loved

01:52:13.900 --> 01:52:18.940
the core four, the four more
spouses, and all the grands,

01:52:18.940 --> 01:52:21.700
just like his own children.

01:52:21.700 --> 01:52:26.050
And she will conclude by sharing
a classic Weasy-dad experience

01:52:26.050 --> 01:52:29.530
in really an iconic
family story.

01:52:29.530 --> 01:52:31.870
Learning to drive with dad
was an intense experience

01:52:31.870 --> 01:52:32.380
at our home.

01:52:32.380 --> 01:52:34.300
This is still Weasy.

01:52:34.300 --> 01:52:36.790
Dad was a secret
car loving junkie

01:52:36.790 --> 01:52:39.190
and he took good
care of his vehicles.

01:52:39.190 --> 01:52:43.270
He was also big on being able
to follow multi-step directions.

01:52:43.270 --> 01:52:47.800
Lastly, he taught that a
driver needs to just drive.

01:52:47.800 --> 01:52:50.200
I think I'd had some
drivers ed classes.

01:52:50.200 --> 01:52:52.780
I was still very new to driving.

01:52:52.780 --> 01:52:55.870
I chose the red BMW to
drive as a learner with dad.

01:52:55.870 --> 01:52:58.030
And we were on the Cape
on a beautiful morning.

01:52:58.030 --> 01:53:00.730
And he said, let's go
for a drive, Louise.

01:53:00.730 --> 01:53:02.080
He didn't call me Weasy.

01:53:02.080 --> 01:53:03.880
He called me Louise.

01:53:03.880 --> 01:53:05.890
So I think, all
right, I have this.

01:53:05.890 --> 01:53:08.016
All my safety nets are in place.

01:53:08.016 --> 01:53:09.640
The biggest of which
were the bracelets

01:53:09.640 --> 01:53:12.190
I wore on my left
arm, many of them

01:53:12.190 --> 01:53:15.460
really almost halfway up my arm.

01:53:15.460 --> 01:53:17.110
They were a tool
that Carol school

01:53:17.110 --> 01:53:22.030
taught me to help remember right
from left as a dyslexic kiddo.

01:53:22.030 --> 01:53:23.420
I got in the car.

01:53:23.420 --> 01:53:25.960
Dad said, you just
need to drive.

01:53:25.960 --> 01:53:28.270
Take off the bracelets.

01:53:28.270 --> 01:53:30.040
I tried to explain my thoughts.

01:53:30.040 --> 01:53:31.900
He said, they're a distraction.

01:53:31.900 --> 01:53:33.910
Just take them off.

01:53:33.910 --> 01:53:36.310
Well, we did OK for a while.

01:53:36.310 --> 01:53:39.340
And then he gave me three
directions in a row to follow.

01:53:39.340 --> 01:53:42.490
Two rights and a left.

01:53:42.490 --> 01:53:45.220
Well, the last left
turned into a right drive

01:53:45.220 --> 01:53:52.306
through a wall of shrubs
into a backyard pool party.

01:53:52.306 --> 01:53:54.621
[LAUGHING]

01:53:55.550 --> 01:53:58.160
I hit the brakes,
thereby causing no damage

01:53:58.160 --> 01:53:59.900
to the guests.

01:53:59.900 --> 01:54:03.722
My father looked at me and told
me to get the hell out of here.

01:54:07.590 --> 01:54:10.000
Instead of driving down the
driveway in front of me,

01:54:10.000 --> 01:54:16.020
I backed out through the shrubs
that I had just driven through.

01:54:16.020 --> 01:54:17.670
We went home immediately.

01:54:17.670 --> 01:54:19.360
We came into the house.

01:54:19.360 --> 01:54:20.910
There was no yelling.

01:54:20.910 --> 01:54:23.010
There was no talking.

01:54:23.010 --> 01:54:25.560
It was still morning.

01:54:25.560 --> 01:54:27.270
Dad went to the
fridge and got a beer.

01:54:33.380 --> 01:54:35.240
I went to the
bathroom and threw up.

01:54:38.460 --> 01:54:40.680
After that, whenever
I went driving,

01:54:40.680 --> 01:54:43.202
dad asked if I had
my bracelets on.

01:54:50.406 --> 01:54:51.405
I did wear my bracelets.

01:54:51.405 --> 01:54:53.760
And from that point
forward, my sister Virginia,

01:54:53.760 --> 01:54:57.860
brother Andrew, and
mom taught me to drive.

01:54:57.860 --> 01:55:01.710
And as Virginia, I'll tell
you that dad did go back

01:55:01.710 --> 01:55:04.890
that afternoon to the house
whose shrubs were damaged

01:55:04.890 --> 01:55:07.740
to make good on the damage.

01:55:07.740 --> 01:55:09.450
Lastly from Louise.

01:55:09.450 --> 01:55:13.290
From September 2014, after my
parents moved to Newbury Court

01:55:13.290 --> 01:55:17.190
till about a year ago, my dad
and I had dates almost every

01:55:17.190 --> 01:55:18.390
Tuesday.

01:55:18.390 --> 01:55:20.970
Sometimes we did errands
for the house and for mom.

01:55:20.970 --> 01:55:22.440
Sometimes we played.

01:55:22.440 --> 01:55:25.177
We loved to walk the paths
on the freedom trails.

01:55:25.177 --> 01:55:27.510
Of course, when you spend the
morning walking the trails

01:55:27.510 --> 01:55:29.480
you need to take
in some yummy food.

01:55:29.480 --> 01:55:31.650
The favorite local
destination for my dad

01:55:31.650 --> 01:55:33.690
was the Colonial Inn in Concord.

01:55:33.690 --> 01:55:36.570
Burger, fries, and
a tall Guinness.

01:55:36.570 --> 01:55:39.480
We developed the term 10 day.

01:55:39.480 --> 01:55:41.570
Dad and I had many 10 days.

01:55:41.570 --> 01:55:44.070
And just for the record,
on our Tuesday date days,

01:55:44.070 --> 01:55:46.590
he told me many times that
I was a very good driver.

01:55:49.860 --> 01:55:52.950
And now my words.

01:55:52.950 --> 01:55:54.940
My father was my hero.

01:55:54.940 --> 01:55:56.970
I looked up to him and
I wanted to be like him

01:55:56.970 --> 01:56:00.570
from the earliest
time I can remember.

01:56:00.570 --> 01:56:03.310
I was always proud
to be his daughter.

01:56:03.310 --> 01:56:05.370
He encouraged and
supported all of us.

01:56:05.370 --> 01:56:07.590
He challenged and stretched us.

01:56:07.590 --> 01:56:10.500
He loved and adored my
mother, my siblings and me

01:56:10.500 --> 01:56:12.120
unconditionally.

01:56:12.120 --> 01:56:15.010
And despite the pressures and
time involved in his work,

01:56:15.010 --> 01:56:17.010
he made himself
emotionally available to us

01:56:17.010 --> 01:56:21.480
through all the changes
and chances of this life.

01:56:21.480 --> 01:56:23.220
All the way through
my high school years,

01:56:23.220 --> 01:56:26.370
I would finish my homework
late, either with him sitting

01:56:26.370 --> 01:56:28.860
in his study or up in my room.

01:56:28.860 --> 01:56:31.710
I always had a check in
time with him before bed.

01:56:31.710 --> 01:56:34.920
He was invariably still up
and working at 11:00 or 12:00

01:56:34.920 --> 01:56:36.120
at night.

01:56:36.120 --> 01:56:38.340
And when he woke
me up at 5:00 AM

01:56:38.340 --> 01:56:42.060
with a very loud electric buzzer
he installed in my third floor

01:56:42.060 --> 01:56:47.400
bedroom, he loved to lean on
that thing, dad, at 5:00 AM,

01:56:47.400 --> 01:56:50.040
was always shaved,
showered, and dressed.

01:56:50.040 --> 01:56:52.090
That was his work ethic.

01:56:52.090 --> 01:56:54.630
He never complained about
his hours or his work.

01:56:54.630 --> 01:56:56.640
It was just what he did.

01:56:56.640 --> 01:56:58.740
He often shared stories
about encounters

01:56:58.740 --> 01:57:01.530
that pleased or
inspired him at MIT,

01:57:01.530 --> 01:57:04.530
or a project he was working
on that excited him,

01:57:04.530 --> 01:57:07.560
or how he won a
squash game that day.

01:57:07.560 --> 01:57:09.720
Daddy was passionate about
his work and the people

01:57:09.720 --> 01:57:11.520
he worked alongside.

01:57:11.520 --> 01:57:15.480
He cared deeply for all the
people God put in his path.

01:57:15.480 --> 01:57:17.790
His principled
honesty, openness,

01:57:17.790 --> 01:57:20.550
and unwavering decency
always on display

01:57:20.550 --> 01:57:24.960
in his deeds and his words were
a life lesson and a life map

01:57:24.960 --> 01:57:26.850
for the four of us.

01:57:26.850 --> 01:57:28.980
My parents welcomed
all comers to our home

01:57:28.980 --> 01:57:30.900
and treated each
visiting student

01:57:30.900 --> 01:57:35.080
or academic like
esteemed family.

01:57:35.080 --> 01:57:36.580
I wanted to be a
doctor of medicine

01:57:36.580 --> 01:57:38.050
when I was in high school.

01:57:38.050 --> 01:57:40.330
In 1975, I graduated
high school and I

01:57:40.330 --> 01:57:42.550
went to a decent
liberal arts college

01:57:42.550 --> 01:57:45.190
and started the
pre-med curriculum.

01:57:45.190 --> 01:57:47.770
I had done well in high
school, but chemistry and math

01:57:47.770 --> 01:57:52.120
had definitely not been
my strong or long suit.

01:57:52.120 --> 01:57:54.970
And yes, you might see
an emerging theme here

01:57:54.970 --> 01:57:57.070
amongst the children.

01:57:57.070 --> 01:58:01.432
The first semester of my
freshman year was a disaster.

01:58:01.432 --> 01:58:03.640
The first failing grades I
had ever earned in my life

01:58:03.640 --> 01:58:05.650
were in that semester.

01:58:05.650 --> 01:58:09.040
I had to drop organic
chemistry and I only

01:58:09.040 --> 01:58:12.190
barely passed calculus because
I rode the Greyhound bus

01:58:12.190 --> 01:58:14.800
home every other weekend
so that dad could help me

01:58:14.800 --> 01:58:17.020
with my problem sets.

01:58:17.020 --> 01:58:19.630
He sat patiently next to me
as I worked the problems,

01:58:19.630 --> 01:58:21.340
never doing them
for me, but helping

01:58:21.340 --> 01:58:24.880
me see the process and
the ways to a solution.

01:58:24.880 --> 01:58:28.540
That year, during Christmas
vacation, dad sat down with me.

01:58:28.540 --> 01:58:32.110
And in the most tender
and loving way imaginable,

01:58:32.110 --> 01:58:35.680
he gently suggested that
I, and I quote him here,

01:58:35.680 --> 01:58:39.700
"make my studies
match my gifts."

01:58:39.700 --> 01:58:41.020
His words.

01:58:41.020 --> 01:58:44.390
I took his advice and moved
in a different direction.

01:58:44.390 --> 01:58:45.970
It made dad smile
when I ended up

01:58:45.970 --> 01:58:49.120
working in the health
ministry for 15 years,

01:58:49.120 --> 01:58:51.670
healing work from a
different perspective.

01:58:51.670 --> 01:58:53.290
I know he was proud of me.

01:58:53.290 --> 01:58:56.110
And he was proud of my husband
and offered so much invaluable

01:58:56.110 --> 01:59:00.220
support and encouragement to
Tom as he worked on his PhD.

01:59:00.220 --> 01:59:04.640
Dad was present when Tom got
his degree in May of 2014.

01:59:04.640 --> 01:59:07.390
And I think dad was more excited
when Tom's book was published

01:59:07.390 --> 01:59:08.740
than we were.

01:59:08.740 --> 01:59:10.300
Dad knew what effort
and commitment

01:59:10.300 --> 01:59:13.480
the degree and the book
required, and he was delighted.

01:59:13.480 --> 01:59:16.570
The fact that the book was a
history book about engineering

01:59:16.570 --> 01:59:19.990
made it kind of perfect.

01:59:19.990 --> 01:59:23.470
I want to end by reflecting
on my father's character.

01:59:23.470 --> 01:59:26.160
His excellent, kind, and
loyal aide [INAUDIBLE]

01:59:26.160 --> 01:59:29.470
stood over his bed two days
before dad died and said,

01:59:29.470 --> 01:59:33.290
quote, "Paul goes
the extra distance."

01:59:33.290 --> 01:59:34.940
Unquote.

01:59:34.940 --> 01:59:37.220
This was true
throughout his life.

01:59:37.220 --> 01:59:41.150
Dad was our very own
resident wise man.

01:59:41.150 --> 01:59:45.310
He could fix any broken thing,
solve any stubborn problem,

01:59:45.310 --> 01:59:48.740
dry a flood of
adolescent girl tears,

01:59:48.740 --> 01:59:51.290
pick the splinters out
of our hands and feet,

01:59:51.290 --> 01:59:54.620
comfort the lovelorn or
lonely, design an adventure

01:59:54.620 --> 01:59:58.530
or plan an amazing trip,
help with our math, science,

01:59:58.530 --> 02:00:00.710
social studies, and
English homework,

02:00:00.710 --> 02:00:04.790
and keep a watchful but low
key eye on our social lives.

02:00:04.790 --> 02:00:07.850
He fought for real people
and authentic inclusion,

02:00:07.850 --> 02:00:10.010
for equity and justice.

02:00:10.010 --> 02:00:13.500
He genuinely cared
about people and causes.

02:00:13.500 --> 02:00:17.360
And he had a sturdy,
steady, solid, no nonsense

02:00:17.360 --> 02:00:20.120
with a twinkle way about him.

02:00:20.120 --> 02:00:22.160
The first time I ever
saw my father cry

02:00:22.160 --> 02:00:26.150
was when the Reverend Dr. Martin
Luther King was assassinated.

02:00:26.150 --> 02:00:28.640
The second time was when he
had to put our first family

02:00:28.640 --> 02:00:30.420
dog down.

02:00:30.420 --> 02:00:35.580
Dad was part terrier, part
bulldog, park Teddy bear.

02:00:35.580 --> 02:00:37.980
He never expected
anything of us that he

02:00:37.980 --> 02:00:39.480
didn't expect of himself.

02:00:39.480 --> 02:00:42.210
And his generosity
knew no limits.

02:00:42.210 --> 02:00:44.850
Dad was both strict and fair.

02:00:44.850 --> 02:00:47.400
He was often reserved,
but his natural way

02:00:47.400 --> 02:00:50.880
of listening more than he
spoke manifested a tender heart

02:00:50.880 --> 02:00:54.990
and an unparalleled knack for
connection and relationship.

02:00:54.990 --> 02:00:57.750
My father was a man who
could be trusted and relied

02:00:57.750 --> 02:01:01.170
upon in every situation
and circumstance of life.

02:01:01.170 --> 02:01:03.060
And as his
granddaughter Caroline

02:01:03.060 --> 02:01:08.370
puts it, in quote, "his
knowledge of all the things."

02:01:11.660 --> 02:01:14.090
Despite dad's success and
steadily advancing career,

02:01:14.090 --> 02:01:16.160
my father was a
deeply humble man.

02:01:16.160 --> 02:01:18.720
He was always more comfortable
in yard work clothes

02:01:18.720 --> 02:01:20.126
than dress up attire.

02:01:20.126 --> 02:01:21.500
And when he was
out in the world,

02:01:21.500 --> 02:01:23.690
he didn't need to
be carefully kept

02:01:23.690 --> 02:01:26.570
and he didn't crave
special attention.

02:01:26.570 --> 02:01:29.000
He also had a vast
appreciation for the people

02:01:29.000 --> 02:01:31.820
who work behind the
scenes to make things go.

02:01:31.820 --> 02:01:34.140
And he was fiercely loyal.

02:01:34.140 --> 02:01:37.060
Two stories demonstrate
these qualities.

02:01:37.060 --> 02:01:39.740
Amy recalls her move in day
at Wheaton College, where

02:01:39.740 --> 02:01:43.130
he had just been appointed
the chairman of the board.

02:01:43.130 --> 02:01:44.570
I quote Amy here.

02:01:44.570 --> 02:01:45.980
"Dad was named
chair of the board

02:01:45.980 --> 02:01:49.130
the summer before I entered
Wheaton as a freshman.

02:01:49.130 --> 02:01:51.320
The day we arrived on
campus to drop me off,

02:01:51.320 --> 02:01:54.080
we pulled into the parking
area closest to Stanton Hall

02:01:54.080 --> 02:01:56.080
where I lived my first year.

02:01:56.080 --> 02:01:58.550
It was not terribly close,
but we were in good company

02:01:58.550 --> 02:02:00.250
and we were managing.

02:02:00.250 --> 02:02:03.320
A security officer recognized
dad and said, Dr. Gray,

02:02:03.320 --> 02:02:05.900
you can pull right up in
front of the building.

02:02:05.900 --> 02:02:08.480
It will be much easier
to unload from there.

02:02:08.480 --> 02:02:11.120
Dad, without a single
pause, said, thank you.

02:02:11.120 --> 02:02:14.100
But we are fine where we are
with all the other families.

02:02:14.100 --> 02:02:18.530
Today, I am the father
of an incoming student."

02:02:18.530 --> 02:02:22.070
Now I quote Martha Millican,
Weasy's childhood BFF

02:02:22.070 --> 02:02:24.620
who is here today.

02:02:24.620 --> 02:02:27.570
"Paul Gray has been a part of
my life for a very long time.

02:02:27.570 --> 02:02:30.350
He was so very gentle and
kind to me growing up.

02:02:30.350 --> 02:02:33.050
My dad wasn't around very
much and doctor and mom G

02:02:33.050 --> 02:02:34.850
took me in as one of their own.

02:02:34.850 --> 02:02:37.400
And boy, could they cook.

02:02:37.400 --> 02:02:40.160
In the early years, Paul would
come pick me up in the morning

02:02:40.160 --> 02:02:43.160
so Weasy and I could go to
school together, hand-in-hand.

02:02:43.160 --> 02:02:45.500
I remember him being concerned
that such little people

02:02:45.500 --> 02:02:47.720
shouldn't walk so far alone.

02:02:47.720 --> 02:02:49.790
I knew I was a lucky girl.

02:02:49.790 --> 02:02:52.280
I was often included in
Gray family vacations.

02:02:52.280 --> 02:02:54.890
Overnights at Sheffield road
had two young girls staying up

02:02:54.890 --> 02:02:57.982
way too late with vast
amount of giggling.

02:02:57.982 --> 02:02:59.440
Overnights at the
president's house

02:02:59.440 --> 02:03:01.070
were always filled
with adventure,

02:03:01.070 --> 02:03:04.220
lots of doors, more good food,
and the all important girl

02:03:04.220 --> 02:03:05.330
time.

02:03:05.330 --> 02:03:06.940
After I graduated
from college, I

02:03:06.940 --> 02:03:08.930
worked for Wellington
Management and MIT

02:03:08.930 --> 02:03:11.350
was one of our firm's first
large institutionalist--

02:03:11.350 --> 02:03:14.750
was our firm's largest
institutional client.

02:03:14.750 --> 02:03:16.760
Whenever Dr. Gray was
coming to Wellington

02:03:16.760 --> 02:03:19.370
for one of the frequent
investment management meetings,

02:03:19.370 --> 02:03:21.920
there would be a
buzz in the building.

02:03:21.920 --> 02:03:24.320
I was not involved in
managing MIT's money,

02:03:24.320 --> 02:03:27.290
but each and every time that
Paul entered the offices,

02:03:27.290 --> 02:03:29.810
he would make a point of
finding me and giving me one

02:03:29.810 --> 02:03:32.270
of those wonderful bear hugs.

02:03:32.270 --> 02:03:34.490
He always made time
to check on me.

02:03:34.490 --> 02:03:37.190
And everybody wondered
why the president of MIT

02:03:37.190 --> 02:03:40.880
was finding Martha, sometimes
bearing flowers or taking her

02:03:40.880 --> 02:03:42.260
out to dinner.

02:03:42.260 --> 02:03:44.750
I knew it was because
I was a lucky girl

02:03:44.750 --> 02:03:48.027
and he was a lovely man."

02:03:48.027 --> 02:03:49.610
There is a view out
there of the world

02:03:49.610 --> 02:03:53.900
that says that whoever we are
is accentuated by our aging.

02:03:53.900 --> 02:03:56.570
I believe that this
is largely true.

02:03:56.570 --> 02:03:58.880
Watching what disease
did to our Father

02:03:58.880 --> 02:04:01.280
over the past seven years is
one of the cruelest things

02:04:01.280 --> 02:04:03.440
that I have ever witnessed.

02:04:03.440 --> 02:04:07.160
However, as Alzheimer's disease
slowly robbed and stripped

02:04:07.160 --> 02:04:11.210
our father of his brilliance,
competence, intellect,

02:04:11.210 --> 02:04:15.440
vast knowledge, problem solving
skills, woodworking gifts,

02:04:15.440 --> 02:04:18.620
and love of reading,
writing, and teaching,

02:04:18.620 --> 02:04:20.780
the essential
daddy or Paul still

02:04:20.780 --> 02:04:24.170
remained with his
dignity intact.

02:04:24.170 --> 02:04:26.870
The man who loved
people, who was

02:04:26.870 --> 02:04:31.340
humble to his very core,
the joyful, gentle man,

02:04:31.340 --> 02:04:33.020
the Teddy bearish
man who trusted

02:04:33.020 --> 02:04:36.630
God, who lit up whenever
his wife, a child,

02:04:36.630 --> 02:04:38.870
including his in-law
children, or a grandchild

02:04:38.870 --> 02:04:40.580
walked into the room.

02:04:40.580 --> 02:04:43.250
The friendly, hospitable,
and open gentleman who gave

02:04:43.250 --> 02:04:45.650
hugs or struck out his
hand, whether or not

02:04:45.650 --> 02:04:48.710
he could remember the person
standing in front of him.

02:04:48.710 --> 02:04:51.890
The sweet and affectionate
father, dedicated mentor,

02:04:51.890 --> 02:04:56.120
and colleague devoted to his
colleagues and his school.

02:04:56.120 --> 02:05:00.140
This man was with us to
the very end of his life.

02:05:00.140 --> 02:05:01.900
Maybe it is impolitic
to say this here

02:05:01.900 --> 02:05:04.060
at MIT, which is an
international leader

02:05:04.060 --> 02:05:07.660
of scientific research, and is
at the forefront of all things

02:05:07.660 --> 02:05:10.420
tech, but I'm going
to say it anyway.

02:05:10.420 --> 02:05:12.490
My father was a man of faith.

02:05:12.490 --> 02:05:15.580
He knew God's grace and
he knew Jesus' name.

02:05:15.580 --> 02:05:17.080
He always thought
that the arguments

02:05:17.080 --> 02:05:21.040
between sacred scripture and
science were a silly tangent.

02:05:21.040 --> 02:05:23.200
Dad perceived the
spark of the divine

02:05:23.200 --> 02:05:26.830
in the work of scientists and
engineers across the Institute,

02:05:26.830 --> 02:05:28.810
and in the best of
human collaboration

02:05:28.810 --> 02:05:33.640
and concentrated effort to
discover, invent, learn, teach,

02:05:33.640 --> 02:05:36.430
share, and advance anew.

02:05:36.430 --> 02:05:39.520
Dad saw the hand of God at work.

02:05:39.520 --> 02:05:41.020
Because he worshiped
a loving God

02:05:41.020 --> 02:05:44.080
and was a keen observer of
human beings and behaviors,

02:05:44.080 --> 02:05:47.930
he also knew that sometimes
we sabotage ourselves.

02:05:47.930 --> 02:05:52.240
Larry pointed out
his email address.

02:05:52.240 --> 02:05:57.260
We have met the enemy and he
is us, Pogo, his favorite tag

02:05:57.260 --> 02:05:58.870
line.

02:05:58.870 --> 02:06:00.610
Dad knew and understood
that to be human

02:06:00.610 --> 02:06:02.440
is to experience a
million Good Friday

02:06:02.440 --> 02:06:07.480
moments, moments of sadness,
challenge, betrayal, hardship,

02:06:07.480 --> 02:06:10.150
suffering, and pain.

02:06:10.150 --> 02:06:13.360
But dad also knew a Resurrection
Jesus, the Lord of love,

02:06:13.360 --> 02:06:16.120
and light, and love--

02:06:16.120 --> 02:06:18.910
love, light, and life.

02:06:18.910 --> 02:06:21.550
The Lord, who revealed by
his living and his dying,

02:06:21.550 --> 02:06:26.350
and by rising again that death
does not have the last word.

02:06:26.350 --> 02:06:28.420
Darkness does not prevail.

02:06:28.420 --> 02:06:31.300
Love always wins.

02:06:31.300 --> 02:06:34.000
There are risks to
being human, accidents

02:06:34.000 --> 02:06:37.000
and illness and tragedy unfold.

02:06:37.000 --> 02:06:39.534
Bodies get old and
disease happens.

02:06:44.460 --> 02:06:48.560
Beloved ones die and
the world seems to shift

02:06:48.560 --> 02:06:51.640
and we wonder if we
can move forward.

02:06:51.640 --> 02:06:56.590
But the truth of my father's
faith is that love never dies.

02:06:56.590 --> 02:07:00.190
Relationship changes
but does not end.

02:07:00.190 --> 02:07:03.290
We carry our deceased loved
ones forward in our hearts

02:07:03.290 --> 02:07:06.790
in the way we live, and
remember, and celebrate.

02:07:06.790 --> 02:07:08.410
We don't say goodbye.

02:07:08.410 --> 02:07:11.710
We say, until we meet again.

02:07:11.710 --> 02:07:14.800
I saw God over and over again
in these last painful years

02:07:14.800 --> 02:07:16.720
of my father's life.

02:07:16.720 --> 02:07:20.950
I saw God in the care and love
our mother extended to dad

02:07:20.950 --> 02:07:25.390
every waking minute,
every single day.

02:07:25.390 --> 02:07:26.920
I saw it in the
care and love that

02:07:26.920 --> 02:07:29.020
this extraordinary
community of MIT

02:07:29.020 --> 02:07:33.550
extended to dad and mom and
us as dad's health failed.

02:07:33.550 --> 02:07:35.860
God was in the small,
intermittent moments

02:07:35.860 --> 02:07:38.650
of connection and spark,
joy and recognition

02:07:38.650 --> 02:07:41.950
that dad savored right
to the end of his life.

02:07:41.950 --> 02:07:45.640
The last week of his life, as
we waited and watched and sat

02:07:45.640 --> 02:07:50.320
vigil, now looks a lot like
a parting gift from dad.

02:07:50.320 --> 02:07:53.800
The gift of connection and
love between our mother,

02:07:53.800 --> 02:07:56.530
the core four,
the four more, and

02:07:56.530 --> 02:07:59.680
the extended and beloved tribe
of grandchildren who are more

02:07:59.680 --> 02:08:02.480
like siblings than cousins.

02:08:02.480 --> 02:08:05.360
Memories and stories,
tears and laughter,

02:08:05.360 --> 02:08:09.680
good food prepared and shared,
favorite stories read aloud,

02:08:09.680 --> 02:08:13.100
and lots of hugs and
hand-holding prepared all of us

02:08:13.100 --> 02:08:15.090
to let go.

02:08:15.090 --> 02:08:18.090
It takes a village
to raise a child.

02:08:18.090 --> 02:08:19.740
It turns out it
also takes a village

02:08:19.740 --> 02:08:23.340
to help an elder die with
dignity and in comfort,

02:08:23.340 --> 02:08:27.000
and to help the bereaved
family move forward.

02:08:27.000 --> 02:08:30.910
You are all key
parts of our village.

02:08:30.910 --> 02:08:33.940
Thank you for all you have
done to support my father

02:08:33.940 --> 02:08:37.750
and mother and our family on
this final leg of my father's

02:08:37.750 --> 02:08:39.340
journey.

02:08:39.340 --> 02:08:42.390
Thank you for the gift of
your presence and esteem,

02:08:42.390 --> 02:08:45.270
for the gift of your
love and devotion.

02:08:45.270 --> 02:08:48.420
Thank you for showing us
love over and over in ways

02:08:48.420 --> 02:08:52.650
that will sustain us and carry
us forward in the days to come.

02:08:52.650 --> 02:08:56.040
May our cherished father
and your cherished leader

02:08:56.040 --> 02:08:57.680
rest in peace.

02:08:57.680 --> 02:09:03.770
And may we honor him,
Paul, dad, Dr. Gray.

02:09:03.770 --> 02:09:06.620
May we honor him and
his legacy by our love,

02:09:06.620 --> 02:09:09.080
by our character
and our compassion,

02:09:09.080 --> 02:09:11.900
by our courage and
our conviction,

02:09:11.900 --> 02:09:15.500
by our willingness to
do good in the world,

02:09:15.500 --> 02:09:19.340
by always telling the
truth, and by our commitment

02:09:19.340 --> 02:09:22.940
to family, community,
honest, hard work,

02:09:22.940 --> 02:09:26.144
and all of God's people.

02:09:26.144 --> 02:09:26.644
Amen.

02:10:03.400 --> 02:10:08.610
I'm Bob Millar, the 11th
chairman of MIT's Corporation.

02:10:08.610 --> 02:10:13.740
Paul's roots at MIT run
deep and they run wide.

02:10:13.740 --> 02:10:16.960
He came here in 1950.

02:10:16.960 --> 02:10:20.040
That was the same
year I was born.

02:10:20.040 --> 02:10:23.610
And from that point forward,
whether as an undergraduate

02:10:23.610 --> 02:10:26.140
or a graduate student, or a
professor, or a chancellor,

02:10:26.140 --> 02:10:31.860
or associate provost, president,
chairman, advisor, or mentor,

02:10:31.860 --> 02:10:38.370
Paul has, had been an integral,
active, and important part

02:10:38.370 --> 02:10:39.315
of MIT's fabric.

02:10:42.220 --> 02:10:45.340
In fact, his presence
here spanned almost half

02:10:45.340 --> 02:10:49.460
of MIT's entire existence.

02:10:49.460 --> 02:10:52.850
And while that statistic
alone is amazing,

02:10:52.850 --> 02:10:55.340
it doesn't begin to
reflect all that Paul

02:10:55.340 --> 02:11:00.460
meant to us through his
friendship and his leadership,

02:11:00.460 --> 02:11:03.980
his wisdom, his foresight,
his energy, and his youth.

02:11:06.850 --> 02:11:10.050
There are some here who may
not have had the joy of knowing

02:11:10.050 --> 02:11:10.920
Paul personally.

02:11:10.920 --> 02:11:14.640
And there are and will
be many, many more

02:11:14.640 --> 02:11:18.980
in the future who will
be denied that honor.

02:11:18.980 --> 02:11:22.500
Nonetheless, we
are and ever will

02:11:22.500 --> 02:11:25.650
be the common beneficiaries
of his good work

02:11:25.650 --> 02:11:27.870
and his good life.

02:11:27.870 --> 02:11:31.050
That is, I believe,
what it means

02:11:31.050 --> 02:11:34.440
to have done so much
to build and nourish

02:11:34.440 --> 02:11:39.060
this great institution of MIT.

02:11:39.060 --> 02:11:40.620
We're grateful
that Paul's family

02:11:40.620 --> 02:11:42.510
continues to be part of MIT.

02:11:42.510 --> 02:11:45.090
And it's an honor to
acknowledge and thank--

02:11:45.090 --> 02:11:45.840
and to thank them.

02:11:45.840 --> 02:11:49.390
Paul's wife, Priscilla,
his son, Andrew,

02:11:49.390 --> 02:11:51.670
and his daughter, Virginia.

02:11:51.670 --> 02:11:56.970
I'd like to thank Rafael,
Shirley, Victor, Jim, Susan,

02:11:56.970 --> 02:12:03.040
Gerald, Andrew, Larry, Virginia,
for their poignant reflections,

02:12:03.040 --> 02:12:07.060
as well as Marcus and David
for the beautiful music.

02:12:07.060 --> 02:12:10.360
I'd also like to thank the
MIT police and the MIT ROTC

02:12:10.360 --> 02:12:13.860
for their presentations
of the flags.

02:12:13.860 --> 02:12:15.990
I'd like to invite all of
you to join Paul's family

02:12:15.990 --> 02:12:18.270
in the Kresge oval
for a reception

02:12:18.270 --> 02:12:22.200
at the conclusion
of this service.

02:12:22.200 --> 02:12:26.160
And I think it's now,
I want to introduce

02:12:26.160 --> 02:12:28.380
the MIT Chorallaries
who will sing the school

02:12:28.380 --> 02:12:30.720
song in Paul's honor.

02:12:30.720 --> 02:12:33.840
And I'd like to thank the
students for being here.

02:12:33.840 --> 02:12:39.324
And invite everyone to join with
them during the second verse.

02:12:39.324 --> 02:12:41.280
Thank you.

02:12:41.280 --> 02:12:44.703
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:12:57.906 --> 02:13:06.495
All rise, all ye of MIT,
and all your fellowship.

02:13:09.310 --> 02:13:15.626
The future beckons unto thee.

02:13:15.626 --> 02:13:18.520
And life is full and rich.

02:13:21.345 --> 02:13:26.834
Arise and raise
your glass on high,

02:13:26.834 --> 02:13:35.080
tonight shall ever
be a memory that

02:13:35.080 --> 02:13:41.130
will never die for ye of MIT.

02:13:44.392 --> 02:13:55.714
Thy sons and daughters, MIT,
return from far and wide.

02:13:55.714 --> 02:14:06.455
And gather here once more to
be renourished by thy side.

02:14:06.455 --> 02:14:13.385
And as we raise
our glasses on high

02:14:13.385 --> 02:14:19.795
to pledge our love
for thee, we join

02:14:19.795 --> 02:14:26.494
all those of days gone
by in praise of MIT.

02:14:39.650 --> 02:14:43.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

